WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.640 --> 00:00:08.039
So today, we're going
to wrap up the lecture

00:00:08.039 --> 00:00:15.560
from last time on stochastic
dynamic programming

00:00:15.560 --> 00:00:18.552
and in particular the infinite
horizon extension to it.

00:00:18.552 --> 00:00:20.760
And then we're going to move
to the next topic, which

00:00:20.760 --> 00:00:25.320
is that of closed loop optimal
control in continuous time,

00:00:25.320 --> 00:00:27.800
which should be a pretty
interesting lecture, as you'll

00:00:27.800 --> 00:00:28.840
see.

00:00:28.840 --> 00:00:31.940
Now, going back to the
discussion from last time,

00:00:31.940 --> 00:00:35.720
the place where we
left it off is we

00:00:35.720 --> 00:00:39.800
defined the extension of
stochastic optimal control

00:00:39.800 --> 00:00:42.297
to an infinite horizon.

00:00:42.297 --> 00:00:42.880
What happened?

00:00:42.880 --> 00:00:45.850
I want to know about a joke.

00:00:45.850 --> 00:00:48.520
I just had restaurants
open on my--

00:00:48.520 --> 00:00:49.620
Four restaurants.

00:00:49.620 --> 00:00:52.160
I hope at least one
of those is Italian.

00:00:52.160 --> 00:00:53.720
[LAUGHTER]

00:00:53.720 --> 00:00:54.440
Is it?

00:00:54.440 --> 00:00:56.800
No.

00:00:56.800 --> 00:00:57.780
Minus 5.

00:00:57.780 --> 00:00:59.290
So it's TA.

00:00:59.290 --> 00:01:00.450
Minus 5 points.

00:01:00.450 --> 00:01:01.250
Anyways.

00:01:01.250 --> 00:01:01.750
All right.

00:01:01.750 --> 00:01:04.810
So we set up the
infinite horizon

00:01:04.810 --> 00:01:08.770
optimal control problem--
infinite horizon MDP problem.

00:01:08.770 --> 00:01:12.770
And then we discussed how the
notion of Bellman equation

00:01:12.770 --> 00:01:18.050
extends to that setting in
a specific form represented

00:01:18.050 --> 00:01:22.370
by the fixed point infinite
horizon Bellman equation

00:01:22.370 --> 00:01:26.350
whereby now the
optimal value function,

00:01:26.350 --> 00:01:28.790
we talked how, in the
infinite horizon setting,

00:01:28.790 --> 00:01:33.450
typically, people written in
terms of rewards as opposed

00:01:33.450 --> 00:01:34.610
to costs.

00:01:34.610 --> 00:01:37.730
So now the object we
are trying to compute

00:01:37.730 --> 00:01:40.970
is not the optimal cost, but the
optimal reward, also referred

00:01:40.970 --> 00:01:43.090
to as optimal value function.

00:01:43.090 --> 00:01:47.850
So the optimal value function
satisfies a fixed point equation

00:01:47.850 --> 00:01:52.610
whereby v star x, the optimal
value for the problem that

00:01:52.610 --> 00:01:57.250
starts at state x is equal
to the maximum over use

00:01:57.250 --> 00:02:04.460
of the immediate reward plus the
average optimal reward to go.

00:02:04.460 --> 00:02:07.340
Where the summation
over T x prime x,

00:02:07.340 --> 00:02:11.140
u is essentially averaging
the optimal value

00:02:11.140 --> 00:02:14.260
function from the
perspective of the next stage

00:02:14.260 --> 00:02:17.300
with respect to all
possible realization

00:02:17.300 --> 00:02:21.780
of the state at the next stage,
given that we are in state x

00:02:21.780 --> 00:02:24.300
and we choose control u.

00:02:24.300 --> 00:02:26.340
And then we saw that
if you are interested

00:02:26.340 --> 00:02:29.120
not in the optimal
value function,

00:02:29.120 --> 00:02:32.060
but in the value of
a given policy pi.

00:02:32.060 --> 00:02:35.460
So basically, how much
reward a given policy pi

00:02:35.460 --> 00:02:37.700
accrues over an
infinite horizon,

00:02:37.700 --> 00:02:42.620
starting from state x, well,
this quantity, referred to as v

00:02:42.620 --> 00:02:46.660
subscript pi of x, can
be computed as a solution

00:02:46.660 --> 00:02:50.900
to the equation
portrayed here, which

00:02:50.900 --> 00:02:54.280
in the case of a
finite state space,

00:02:54.280 --> 00:02:56.950
amounts to solving a
linear system of equations

00:02:56.950 --> 00:03:00.370
into the components of
the value function vector.

00:03:00.370 --> 00:03:06.710
Remember that v of x is the
value for a specific state x.

00:03:06.710 --> 00:03:08.850
And if you have 10
possible states,

00:03:08.850 --> 00:03:11.230
then you will have
10 different values,

00:03:11.230 --> 00:03:13.870
one for each possible
value of the state.

00:03:13.870 --> 00:03:16.710
Does that make sense?

00:03:16.710 --> 00:03:19.390
And then the additional
twist that we introduced

00:03:19.390 --> 00:03:24.030
last time is, what if we
introduce the notion of a Q

00:03:24.030 --> 00:03:28.230
function that conceptually
takes on the-- capture

00:03:28.230 --> 00:03:32.790
the concept of starting
at x using control u,

00:03:32.790 --> 00:03:37.150
and then using the optimal
policy from the next stage

00:03:37.150 --> 00:03:39.190
onward?

00:03:39.190 --> 00:03:41.070
If that is the case,
so if we reason

00:03:41.070 --> 00:03:44.350
in terms of the Q
function, Q star function,

00:03:44.350 --> 00:03:47.390
then the Q star
function actually

00:03:47.390 --> 00:03:51.630
satisfies an
equation that is very

00:03:51.630 --> 00:03:56.090
similar to the Bellman
equation, whereby the optimal Q

00:03:56.090 --> 00:03:59.370
function is equal to
the current reward

00:03:59.370 --> 00:04:05.250
plus the average of
the maximum Q function

00:04:05.250 --> 00:04:09.250
maximize over u prime.

00:04:09.250 --> 00:04:12.310
We derived this
equation last time.

00:04:12.310 --> 00:04:15.530
So I'm not going to
go through it again.

00:04:15.530 --> 00:04:18.450
But the bottom line is that if
you have an infinite horizon

00:04:18.450 --> 00:04:22.890
MDP, then we have an analog
of the Bellman equation

00:04:22.890 --> 00:04:24.570
in the infinite
horizon setting, which

00:04:24.570 --> 00:04:27.130
is represented by a
fixed point equation,

00:04:27.130 --> 00:04:29.750
either for the value function
or for the Q function.

00:04:29.750 --> 00:04:33.010
We discussed last time what
the notion of Q function

00:04:33.010 --> 00:04:35.790
is particularly useful
in learning settings.

00:04:35.790 --> 00:04:40.250
We'll come back to it
in [INAUDIBLE] lectures.

00:04:40.250 --> 00:04:43.170
So now the next
question is, how do we

00:04:43.170 --> 00:04:46.230
solve the fixed point equation?

00:04:46.230 --> 00:04:50.450
So this is an equation
in which the unknown

00:04:50.450 --> 00:04:54.060
is the function V of x.

00:04:54.060 --> 00:04:56.860
We know R because we specified.

00:04:56.860 --> 00:04:59.500
We are in a setting whereby
we assume that we know

00:04:59.500 --> 00:05:01.160
the transition probabilities.

00:05:01.160 --> 00:05:04.800
So we know R and T, but
we don't know V star.

00:05:04.800 --> 00:05:08.980
So how do we solve
for V star or, how

00:05:08.980 --> 00:05:10.880
do we solve for
the optimal policy?

00:05:10.880 --> 00:05:14.162
I mean, in general, you
are interested in the value

00:05:14.162 --> 00:05:15.620
function, but most
importantly, you

00:05:15.620 --> 00:05:17.220
are interested in
the optimal policy

00:05:17.220 --> 00:05:20.180
because that's the
object you use in order

00:05:20.180 --> 00:05:21.600
to control your system.

00:05:25.100 --> 00:05:31.100
So, at a very high level, there
are two settings that we're

00:05:31.100 --> 00:05:32.480
going to address in this class.

00:05:32.480 --> 00:05:34.155
And both are
extremely important.

00:05:34.155 --> 00:05:35.780
The first setting,
which is the setting

00:05:35.780 --> 00:05:37.440
of the remainder
of this lecture,

00:05:37.440 --> 00:05:40.420
is the setting where we
know the transition function

00:05:40.420 --> 00:05:42.700
T. It has been given to us.

00:05:42.700 --> 00:05:44.540
We know the reward function.

00:05:44.540 --> 00:05:48.140
So in this setting where all
the problem data is known,

00:05:48.140 --> 00:05:52.190
the only unknown is the value
function and the optimal policy.

00:05:52.190 --> 00:05:54.630
There are two broad
classes of algorithms,

00:05:54.630 --> 00:05:57.430
one referred to as value
iteration and the other one

00:05:57.430 --> 00:06:03.060
referred to as policy iteration.

00:06:03.060 --> 00:06:05.310
It's important you familiarize
with these two concepts

00:06:05.310 --> 00:06:08.310
because they've become
especially important as we

00:06:08.310 --> 00:06:10.710
move to the learning setting.

00:06:10.710 --> 00:06:12.610
Specifically in the
learning setting,

00:06:12.610 --> 00:06:17.030
some of the problems in data
is not assumed to be known.

00:06:17.030 --> 00:06:20.110
For example, we may
not know or have

00:06:20.110 --> 00:06:25.190
direct access to the
transition probabilities T.

00:06:25.190 --> 00:06:28.830
So how do we solve for
the value function?

00:06:28.830 --> 00:06:31.150
And how do we solve
for the optimal policy?

00:06:31.150 --> 00:06:34.710
There are, essentially,
two types of methods.

00:06:34.710 --> 00:06:37.710
The first type is referred
to as model-based.

00:06:37.710 --> 00:06:42.010
The idea is that you infer
from data all the problem data,

00:06:42.010 --> 00:06:43.890
for example, the
transition probabilities.

00:06:43.890 --> 00:06:48.670
And once you've done so,
then you're back into this

00:06:48.670 --> 00:06:51.060
setting where all the
problem data is known.

00:06:51.060 --> 00:06:53.800
And then you can use value
iteration or policy iteration.

00:06:53.800 --> 00:06:58.560
Or you just go directly
to the quantity

00:06:58.560 --> 00:07:00.860
that you care about, to
the optimal value function

00:07:00.860 --> 00:07:02.100
and the optimal policy.

00:07:02.100 --> 00:07:05.560
Those methods are
referred to as model-free.

00:07:05.560 --> 00:07:09.520
So in this class, we will cover
both model-based and model-free

00:07:09.520 --> 00:07:10.160
methods.

00:07:10.160 --> 00:07:13.080
They have different
pros and cons.

00:07:13.080 --> 00:07:17.400
We'll cover those in two
to three weeks from now.

00:07:17.400 --> 00:07:21.160
But for now, we focus on the
first setting, the one where

00:07:21.160 --> 00:07:23.960
all the problem data is known.

00:07:23.960 --> 00:07:26.120
And the algorithms,
as we will see,

00:07:26.120 --> 00:07:28.960
are relatively
simple conceptually.

00:07:28.960 --> 00:07:33.060
So let's start with the
value iteration algorithm.

00:07:33.060 --> 00:07:37.120
The value iteration
algorithm is quite intuitive.

00:07:37.120 --> 00:07:42.280
The idea is that you set up an
iteration where you iteratively

00:07:42.280 --> 00:07:44.920
reoptimize the fixed
endpoint Bellman

00:07:44.920 --> 00:07:48.280
equation, in a style that is
very similar to the Bellman

00:07:48.280 --> 00:07:51.370
equation we saw in
discrete time setting.

00:07:51.370 --> 00:07:54.130
You start from an
initial condition

00:07:54.130 --> 00:07:56.830
for the value function,
maybe just the zero vector.

00:07:56.830 --> 00:08:01.130
So you assume that the value
for each possible state

00:08:01.130 --> 00:08:02.570
is equal to 0.

00:08:02.570 --> 00:08:06.810
You apply one step Bellman
equation for many, many times,

00:08:06.810 --> 00:08:09.570
and then you hope that you
will converge to something.

00:08:12.290 --> 00:08:15.050
It turns out that this
algorithm is indeed

00:08:15.050 --> 00:08:19.010
guaranteed to converge to the
optimal value function V star.

00:08:19.010 --> 00:08:26.410
So for any initial condition,
the iterates V case

00:08:26.410 --> 00:08:31.210
will converge to the
optimal V star of x.

00:08:31.210 --> 00:08:34.789
If you have no clue about
what the value function is,

00:08:34.789 --> 00:08:39.890
you can just start by assigning
0 to all possible states.

00:08:39.890 --> 00:08:42.169
Of course, if you
have some guess

00:08:42.169 --> 00:08:46.590
about what the optimal
value function is,

00:08:46.590 --> 00:08:48.140
you should use that guess.

00:08:48.140 --> 00:08:50.320
You're guaranteed
to converge anyways.

00:08:50.320 --> 00:08:52.340
But of course, the
convergence rate

00:08:52.340 --> 00:08:56.220
is much faster if you
start from a good guess.

00:08:56.220 --> 00:08:59.560
So what is the intuition
behind the value iteration?

00:08:59.560 --> 00:09:02.500
Remember that we are an
infinite horizon setting.

00:09:02.500 --> 00:09:07.500
So let's assume that
you start at infinity.

00:09:07.500 --> 00:09:10.860
And then assume-- OK,
let me rephrase it.

00:09:10.860 --> 00:09:13.820
Let's assume that you, instead
of having an infinite horizon

00:09:13.820 --> 00:09:16.140
problem, you have
a finite horizon

00:09:16.140 --> 00:09:18.140
problem where the
horizon is very, very

00:09:18.140 --> 00:09:20.740
long, like 3 trillion years.

00:09:20.740 --> 00:09:23.820
Then you start from 3
trillion years in the future,

00:09:23.820 --> 00:09:26.900
and then you start doing
a backward recursion

00:09:26.900 --> 00:09:29.660
many, many times until today.

00:09:29.660 --> 00:09:33.380
Well, that really approximates
the infinite horizon setting.

00:09:33.380 --> 00:09:36.180
And that's the intuition
behind the value iteration

00:09:36.180 --> 00:09:39.300
algorithms for the
infinite horizon problem.

00:09:39.300 --> 00:09:42.745
And it turns out that in the
stationary setting, once you

00:09:42.745 --> 00:09:44.620
apply the Bellman
equation, many, many times,

00:09:44.620 --> 00:09:46.537
you converge to a steady
state solution, which

00:09:46.537 --> 00:09:48.510
is the solution to the
fixed point equation

00:09:48.510 --> 00:09:50.790
that I showed before.

00:09:50.790 --> 00:09:54.272
Does it actually make sense?

00:09:54.272 --> 00:09:56.730
Of course, there should be a
proof to prove this statement.

00:09:56.730 --> 00:09:57.813
It's not entirely obvious.

00:09:57.813 --> 00:09:59.930
I'm just giving you the
high-level intuition.

00:09:59.930 --> 00:10:00.550
Yeah.

00:10:00.550 --> 00:10:02.830
So are you saying that
this is essentially

00:10:02.830 --> 00:10:06.310
analogous to what dynamic
programming [INAUDIBLE]

00:10:06.310 --> 00:10:07.010
very long?

00:10:07.010 --> 00:10:07.190
Yeah.

00:10:07.190 --> 00:10:09.350
So we spent half an hour to
explain the infinite horizon

00:10:09.350 --> 00:10:09.690
setting.

00:10:09.690 --> 00:10:11.982
By the end of the day, we're
still doing finite horizon

00:10:11.982 --> 00:10:12.570
computations.

00:10:12.570 --> 00:10:14.190
Yes.

00:10:14.190 --> 00:10:16.270
But if you do it many,
many times, then you're

00:10:16.270 --> 00:10:18.150
guaranteed to converge
to the solution

00:10:18.150 --> 00:10:20.750
for the infinite
horizon setting.

00:10:20.750 --> 00:10:22.870
Yep, that's exactly right.

00:10:22.870 --> 00:10:26.150
And of course, once you have
computed the optimal value

00:10:26.150 --> 00:10:30.510
function, then you can
backtrack the optimal control

00:10:30.510 --> 00:10:36.690
by just solving for the
maximization over the use.

00:10:36.690 --> 00:10:38.250
And now you know V star.

00:10:38.250 --> 00:10:41.767
So actually, this optimization
problem can be solved.

00:10:41.767 --> 00:10:43.350
[INAUDIBLE] in
advance, how many times

00:10:43.350 --> 00:10:46.720
you have to repeat [INAUDIBLE]?

00:10:46.720 --> 00:10:48.600
No, unfortunately,
it's asymptotic.

00:10:48.600 --> 00:10:51.280
We'll come with-- I'll show
you another algorithm that

00:10:51.280 --> 00:10:57.640
in some cases actually has
a finite horizon convergence

00:10:57.640 --> 00:10:59.080
guarantees.

00:10:59.080 --> 00:11:01.560
So then you are raising
a good point, though.

00:11:01.560 --> 00:11:02.700
When do we stop?

00:11:07.080 --> 00:11:10.000
What is it?

00:11:10.000 --> 00:11:12.000
When do you stop
value iteration?

00:11:12.000 --> 00:11:13.660
In a way, it's similar to--

00:11:16.662 --> 00:11:18.120
it's similar to
the situation where

00:11:18.120 --> 00:11:21.700
we're doing gradient-based
methods for optimization.

00:11:21.700 --> 00:11:25.400
So for example, if the
difference between the Vk and Vk

00:11:25.400 --> 00:11:29.000
plus 1 across all of the
states is not too large,

00:11:29.000 --> 00:11:31.120
that could be a good
termination criterion,

00:11:31.120 --> 00:11:35.020
or maybe after an arbitrary
number of iterations, say 2,000,

00:11:35.020 --> 00:11:36.240
just call it a day.

00:11:36.240 --> 00:11:41.340
So there are different ways
to terminate the algorithm.

00:11:41.340 --> 00:11:45.220
There are, of course, a bunch of
much more sophisticated analysis

00:11:45.220 --> 00:11:47.140
that also give
you an error bound

00:11:47.140 --> 00:11:50.380
in terms of how much you might
be far off the optimal value

00:11:50.380 --> 00:11:52.700
function after a
certain number of steps.

00:11:52.700 --> 00:11:55.825
But in general,
termination is not obvious.

00:11:55.825 --> 00:11:58.200
But you're guaranteed that if
you do it many, many times,

00:11:58.200 --> 00:12:00.420
you converge to the
value function--

00:12:00.420 --> 00:12:03.100
optimal value function.

00:12:03.100 --> 00:12:05.060
And there is an
analog of it also

00:12:05.060 --> 00:12:08.940
for Q functions is essentially
taking the fixed point

00:12:08.940 --> 00:12:10.580
equation for the
optimal Q function

00:12:10.580 --> 00:12:16.980
and then iterate
analogous intuition.

00:12:16.980 --> 00:12:17.880
Policy iteration.

00:12:17.880 --> 00:12:19.740
I'm not spending too much time
in value iteration and policy

00:12:19.740 --> 00:12:22.420
iteration because we will
revisit these two algorithms

00:12:22.420 --> 00:12:24.540
through a learning-based
lens, which is ultimately

00:12:24.540 --> 00:12:26.980
what we are mostly
interested in, in this class.

00:12:26.980 --> 00:12:31.460
For now, I'm just giving you the
overarching ideas and concepts

00:12:31.460 --> 00:12:33.100
and terminology.

00:12:33.100 --> 00:12:35.560
Policy iteration takes
a different approach.

00:12:35.560 --> 00:12:38.500
Rather than operating
in value space that

00:12:38.500 --> 00:12:42.230
is optimizing over the
value functions themselves,

00:12:42.230 --> 00:12:47.790
it operates in a policy space
that is optimized directly

00:12:47.790 --> 00:12:50.330
in the space of policies,
which can make sense

00:12:50.330 --> 00:12:51.830
because at the end
of the day, we're

00:12:51.830 --> 00:12:55.610
mostly interested in the
optimal [INAUDIBLE] policy.

00:12:55.610 --> 00:12:59.630
The value function is more
of an intermediary concept

00:12:59.630 --> 00:13:03.230
that allows us to compute
the optimal policy.

00:13:03.230 --> 00:13:06.110
So the idea of a policy
evaluation is as follows.

00:13:06.110 --> 00:13:08.210
It's still an
iterative algorithm.

00:13:08.210 --> 00:13:10.270
So you start from
an initial condition

00:13:10.270 --> 00:13:15.730
and then you apply your insight
into MDP and optimal control.

00:13:15.730 --> 00:13:18.150
And you can try to come
up with a better policy.

00:13:18.150 --> 00:13:21.510
And then you continue
iterating until you

00:13:21.510 --> 00:13:22.870
get into a good policy.

00:13:22.870 --> 00:13:24.910
Specifically, how does it work?

00:13:24.910 --> 00:13:28.750
Let's assume that you start
from a given policy pi k.

00:13:28.750 --> 00:13:35.070
So here k does not
refer to the time stage,

00:13:35.070 --> 00:13:38.010
but is the iteration counter.

00:13:43.160 --> 00:13:46.760
So k stands for the kth
iteration in the policy

00:13:46.760 --> 00:13:48.480
iteration algorithm.

00:13:48.480 --> 00:13:50.500
So you start from a policy pi k.

00:13:57.040 --> 00:14:02.960
Then what the policy iteration
algorithm does is a 2-step

00:14:02.960 --> 00:14:08.760
procedure where first, we
compute the value of the policy

00:14:08.760 --> 00:14:16.240
pi k for each possible state x
by using the Bellman equation

00:14:16.240 --> 00:14:20.000
that allows you to
derive the value of--

00:14:20.000 --> 00:14:23.560
for the value function
for a given policy pi k.

00:14:23.560 --> 00:14:27.460
So you compute V of pi k.

00:14:31.360 --> 00:14:34.160
This is the same equation I
had in the previous slides.

00:14:34.160 --> 00:14:37.080
Note that here I'm computing
the value associated

00:14:37.080 --> 00:14:38.140
with specific policy.

00:14:38.140 --> 00:14:39.390
So there is no optimization.

00:14:39.390 --> 00:14:41.490
There is no maximization
with respect to u.

00:14:41.490 --> 00:14:46.370
So this equation, at least
notionally, is easy to solve.

00:14:46.370 --> 00:14:49.870
We know R. We know the
transition probabilities.

00:14:49.870 --> 00:14:51.290
This is just numbers.

00:14:51.290 --> 00:14:54.490
So this is just a
linear equations

00:14:54.490 --> 00:14:57.690
in the value function V pi.

00:14:57.690 --> 00:15:01.130
So you could solve it as a
large scale linear system

00:15:01.130 --> 00:15:03.850
in the unknowns
that are represented

00:15:03.850 --> 00:15:06.930
by the value of the
policy when starting

00:15:06.930 --> 00:15:10.030
from each possible state
x, x equal 1, x equal 2,

00:15:10.030 --> 00:15:11.910
and so on and so forth.

00:15:11.910 --> 00:15:12.410
Yeah.

00:15:12.410 --> 00:15:16.050
[INAUDIBLE] on the right
side of the first one V pi k

00:15:16.050 --> 00:15:19.930
also [INAUDIBLE].

00:15:19.930 --> 00:15:21.190
Yeah, absolutely.

00:15:21.190 --> 00:15:21.930
That's a typo.

00:15:21.930 --> 00:15:24.850
[INAUDIBLE]

00:15:26.770 --> 00:15:28.210
Here, no.

00:15:28.210 --> 00:15:30.390
So here that's not a typo.

00:15:35.730 --> 00:15:37.390
Here is exactly
what we had here.

00:15:37.390 --> 00:15:41.020
So if you are given a
policy pi, the value--

00:15:41.020 --> 00:15:46.540
so the cumulative sum of rewards
discounted with a discounting

00:15:46.540 --> 00:15:50.960
factor gamma is given by
solving the equation here.

00:15:56.060 --> 00:15:58.740
How can the sum looks
like the rigt-hand side

00:15:58.740 --> 00:16:01.492
depends on the left-hand side.

00:16:01.492 --> 00:16:03.440
Yeah, these are linear
systems of equation.

00:16:03.440 --> 00:16:07.320
So you would collect everything
on the left-hand side.

00:16:07.320 --> 00:16:08.740
Here you have your
constant terms

00:16:08.740 --> 00:16:11.980
and then you will
solve for the vector V,

00:16:11.980 --> 00:16:19.460
V of x is essentially a
vector is v of 0, v of--

00:16:19.460 --> 00:16:22.820
I mean, assume that x
can take on four values--

00:16:22.820 --> 00:16:25.980
0, 1, 2, and 3.

00:16:25.980 --> 00:16:30.720
It will be V of 0, V
of 1, V of 2, V of 3.

00:16:33.460 --> 00:16:34.280
Make sense?

00:16:34.280 --> 00:16:35.760
This makes it a
bit more explicit.

00:16:40.300 --> 00:16:44.910
So after you have done that,
then you had a big step.

00:16:44.910 --> 00:16:47.910
So the first step is referred
to as policy evaluation

00:16:47.910 --> 00:16:50.790
because we are evaluating the
value associated to a given

00:16:50.790 --> 00:16:52.110
policy pi.

00:16:52.110 --> 00:16:57.110
The policy improvement step
is where the magic happens.

00:16:57.110 --> 00:17:05.670
So now that we have computed
a value for a given policy pi,

00:17:05.670 --> 00:17:08.270
we try to come up
with a better policy.

00:17:08.270 --> 00:17:12.510
And to do so, we
apply a one-step

00:17:12.510 --> 00:17:14.930
of the Bellman equation.

00:17:14.930 --> 00:17:22.109
So we set the value-- we set the
control for the revised policy

00:17:22.109 --> 00:17:23.589
p or k plus 1.

00:17:23.589 --> 00:17:26.510
This will be iteration k plus 1.

00:17:26.510 --> 00:17:30.830
As the control, the
maximize, since we

00:17:30.830 --> 00:17:35.140
are in the reward setting,
the stage-wise reward,

00:17:35.140 --> 00:17:40.920
so the current reward
plus the reward

00:17:40.920 --> 00:17:50.580
starting from the next stage
approximated by V pi k.

00:17:55.000 --> 00:17:58.160
So basically, we take a
one-step Bellman equation

00:17:58.160 --> 00:18:01.560
to try to improve
the action that I

00:18:01.560 --> 00:18:04.580
should be taking at state x.

00:18:09.080 --> 00:18:13.680
So this is not the
optimal action.

00:18:13.680 --> 00:18:15.880
The optimal action
will be the one

00:18:15.880 --> 00:18:17.440
that you will be
computing if you

00:18:17.440 --> 00:18:22.440
were to plug in V star,
the optimal value function.

00:18:22.440 --> 00:18:26.240
But you use V pi k as
a sort of placeholder

00:18:26.240 --> 00:18:29.760
that hopefully allows you
to derive a better action.

00:18:29.760 --> 00:18:32.840
The optimal action is the
one that you will compute

00:18:32.840 --> 00:18:35.170
if you were to use V star.

00:18:35.170 --> 00:18:38.570
We're not using V star
because we know V star yet.

00:18:38.570 --> 00:18:43.450
We use a surrogate,
which is Vpk.

00:18:43.450 --> 00:18:47.530
So the key result,
and of course, there

00:18:47.530 --> 00:18:49.850
should be a proof
here, is not entirely

00:18:49.850 --> 00:18:54.090
obvious, is that the
value for the new policy

00:18:54.090 --> 00:18:57.630
is greater than or equal to the
value for the previous policy.

00:18:57.630 --> 00:19:02.510
So actually, you are getting
into a policy that is better.

00:19:02.510 --> 00:19:04.190
Remember that we're
in a reward setting.

00:19:04.190 --> 00:19:08.970
So the higher the value,
the happier you are.

00:19:08.970 --> 00:19:15.590
And even better, the inequality
is strict if pi k is suboptimal,

00:19:15.590 --> 00:19:18.490
it's not the optimal policy.

00:19:18.490 --> 00:19:20.570
If you find the
optimal policy, great.

00:19:20.570 --> 00:19:23.470
If you have not found optimal
policy, you know the policy,

00:19:23.470 --> 00:19:27.170
the next iteration is
going to be better.

00:19:27.170 --> 00:19:29.490
So going back to the
question from your colleague

00:19:29.490 --> 00:19:35.070
about finite time
guarantees, assume that you

00:19:35.070 --> 00:19:37.750
have a finite number of states.

00:19:37.750 --> 00:19:40.950
So x can take on the
value 0, 1, 2, 3, 4,

00:19:40.950 --> 00:19:46.110
and a finite number of controls.

00:19:46.110 --> 00:19:48.110
Do you think that this
algorithm, knowing

00:19:48.110 --> 00:19:52.310
this property, do you think
that this algorithm is

00:19:52.310 --> 00:19:55.130
going to converge in a
finite number of steps?

00:20:00.030 --> 00:20:02.790
Yes or no?

00:20:02.790 --> 00:20:06.310
Yes, because essentially, if you
have a finite number of states--

00:20:06.310 --> 00:20:08.430
a finite number
of controls, there

00:20:08.430 --> 00:20:12.190
are finitely many
policies, different ways

00:20:12.190 --> 00:20:14.150
that you can permute
the control with respect

00:20:14.150 --> 00:20:15.750
to all the different states.

00:20:15.750 --> 00:20:19.430
Since you know that at
every step of this algorithm

00:20:19.430 --> 00:20:24.590
you are always going to strictly
improve the value of a given

00:20:24.590 --> 00:20:27.430
policy, there are so
many times that you

00:20:27.430 --> 00:20:30.800
can improve the value until
you reach the optimal value.

00:20:30.800 --> 00:20:33.200
So indeed, policy
iteration converges

00:20:33.200 --> 00:20:37.020
to a finite number of steps in
the setting of a fixed number--

00:20:37.020 --> 00:20:42.560
I mean, finite number of states
and finite number of controls.

00:20:42.560 --> 00:20:44.480
Of course, what
you should prove--

00:20:44.480 --> 00:20:47.560
and again, all these
proofs are provided

00:20:47.560 --> 00:20:50.320
in great detail in the
books from Bertsekas

00:20:50.320 --> 00:20:54.300
is the monotonic improvement
property we have here.

00:20:58.960 --> 00:21:00.960
So, in a nutshell,
with value iteration,

00:21:00.960 --> 00:21:03.420
we estimate the
optimal value function,

00:21:03.420 --> 00:21:06.960
and we can compute
the optimal policy

00:21:06.960 --> 00:21:10.600
through the Bellman equation
once we know the optimal value

00:21:10.600 --> 00:21:11.560
function.

00:21:11.560 --> 00:21:14.080
In policy iteration, we
start with a random policy

00:21:14.080 --> 00:21:17.160
and we iteratively improve
it until convergence

00:21:17.160 --> 00:21:18.840
to an optimal policy.

00:21:18.840 --> 00:21:23.720
Of course, all of this requires
the model of MDP to be known.

00:21:23.720 --> 00:21:29.370
In particular, it assumes
that we know the transition

00:21:29.370 --> 00:21:30.970
probabilities here.

00:21:30.970 --> 00:21:32.650
Otherwise, for
example, you wouldn't

00:21:32.650 --> 00:21:38.490
be able to solve for
the V pi k quantities.

00:21:38.490 --> 00:21:39.170
Yeah.

00:21:39.170 --> 00:21:45.090
Explain why, typically, the
policy should improve each step?

00:21:45.090 --> 00:21:49.290
Well, I mean, you are making
essentially a more informed

00:21:49.290 --> 00:21:53.710
decision that exploit the
structure of the problem,

00:21:53.710 --> 00:21:57.130
where you not only look at
the immediate gains, but also

00:21:57.130 --> 00:21:58.610
the future gains.

00:21:58.610 --> 00:22:01.130
And this is
essentially a surrogate

00:22:01.130 --> 00:22:03.730
of what those future
gains could look at.

00:22:03.730 --> 00:22:07.650
So it's not 100% clear
what that necessarily

00:22:07.650 --> 00:22:09.050
brings an improvement.

00:22:09.050 --> 00:22:11.790
But I think that intuitively
stands to reason.

00:22:18.470 --> 00:22:18.970
Great.

00:22:18.970 --> 00:22:22.650
So this is all I want to say
about the infinite horizon MDPs.

00:22:22.650 --> 00:22:24.850
We will revisit
them at great length

00:22:24.850 --> 00:22:27.820
when we talk about
learning-based control.

00:22:27.820 --> 00:22:33.020
So my main goal here would
just to set the terminology

00:22:33.020 --> 00:22:37.060
for the infinite
horizon instantiation

00:22:37.060 --> 00:22:40.060
of stochastic optimal
control problems

00:22:40.060 --> 00:22:43.220
and really fix some
foundational ideas in terms

00:22:43.220 --> 00:22:47.060
of how the Bellman equation
applies to an infinite horizon

00:22:47.060 --> 00:22:49.700
setting and how we can
solve for it through two

00:22:49.700 --> 00:22:54.100
main methodologies, namely value
iteration and policy iteration.

00:22:54.100 --> 00:22:59.780
We'll come back to all of
these in three weeks or so.

00:22:59.780 --> 00:23:01.740
Instead, what we're
going to do now

00:23:01.740 --> 00:23:06.060
is we go back to the
deterministic setting,

00:23:06.060 --> 00:23:10.140
but we explore the
complementary dimension whereby

00:23:10.140 --> 00:23:13.520
instead of reasoning
in finite time,

00:23:13.520 --> 00:23:15.400
we reason in continuous time.

00:23:20.500 --> 00:23:22.600
So going back to our agenda.

00:23:22.600 --> 00:23:28.110
So we have discussed
at length this branch.

00:23:28.110 --> 00:23:31.610
So finite horizon, closed
loop optimal control

00:23:31.610 --> 00:23:36.590
through the lens of the Bellman
equation in dynamic programming.

00:23:36.590 --> 00:23:39.190
We have looked at
specific instantiations

00:23:39.190 --> 00:23:46.550
of the ideas of dynamic
programming, including LQR, RQR.

00:23:46.550 --> 00:23:49.150
So now we are moving
to the setting

00:23:49.150 --> 00:23:51.550
where by now time is
a continuous variable.

00:23:51.550 --> 00:23:54.070
So this is useful because
sometimes problems

00:23:54.070 --> 00:23:57.250
are more naturally framed
in continuous time.

00:23:57.250 --> 00:23:58.790
And sometimes the
policies that are

00:23:58.790 --> 00:24:01.070
derived from the
continuous time formulation

00:24:01.070 --> 00:24:04.410
are a bit cleaner and also
a bit easier to implement.

00:24:04.410 --> 00:24:07.230
So we'll see.

00:24:07.230 --> 00:24:11.675
And so what we will derive is
the continuous time extension

00:24:11.675 --> 00:24:13.550
of the dynamic programming
equations referred

00:24:13.550 --> 00:24:17.670
to as Hamilton-Jacobi-Bellman
and Hamilton-Jacobi-Isaacs

00:24:17.670 --> 00:24:18.870
equation.

00:24:18.870 --> 00:24:22.590
And then we're going to use
those techniques to solve

00:24:22.590 --> 00:24:25.040
the two problems of importance.

00:24:25.040 --> 00:24:26.283
LQR and continuous time.

00:24:26.283 --> 00:24:27.700
This looks like
all these classes,

00:24:27.700 --> 00:24:29.720
there's one problem
we can solve this LQR.

00:24:29.720 --> 00:24:31.075
LQR is also a pedagogical.

00:24:31.075 --> 00:24:31.700
It's important.

00:24:31.700 --> 00:24:34.000
It's also a pedagogical tool.

00:24:34.000 --> 00:24:36.520
And then another very
interesting class of problems

00:24:36.520 --> 00:24:38.140
related to
reachability analysis.

00:24:38.140 --> 00:24:39.120
Actually this is--

00:24:39.120 --> 00:24:40.422
I mean, LQR is very important.

00:24:40.422 --> 00:24:41.880
Reachability analysis
is also quite

00:24:41.880 --> 00:24:48.080
important is what we used,
for example, to devise

00:24:48.080 --> 00:24:52.840
collision avoidance policies for
autonomous cars or for airplanes

00:24:52.840 --> 00:24:53.940
and so on and so forth.

00:24:57.840 --> 00:25:00.320
So the previous lecture, we
focused on the discrete time

00:25:00.320 --> 00:25:00.820
setting.

00:25:00.820 --> 00:25:04.620
In this lecture, we focus on
the continuous time setting.

00:25:04.620 --> 00:25:07.400
We will discuss how the dynamic
programming approach leads

00:25:07.400 --> 00:25:09.765
to its continuous
time counterpart,

00:25:09.765 --> 00:25:11.890
referred to as the
Hamilton-Jacobi-Bellman equation

00:25:11.890 --> 00:25:14.160
and the Hamilton-Jacobi-Isaacs
equation.

00:25:14.160 --> 00:25:16.600
We'll use the
Hamilton-Jacobi-Bellman equation

00:25:16.600 --> 00:25:20.080
to solve the LQR problem
in continuous time,

00:25:20.080 --> 00:25:23.970
and then we'll use the
Hamilton-Jacobi-Isaacs equations

00:25:23.970 --> 00:25:26.850
to address the reachability
analysis problem

00:25:26.850 --> 00:25:30.050
that we'll define later on.

00:25:30.050 --> 00:25:32.890
So just to make sure that
we are on the same page.

00:25:32.890 --> 00:25:35.770
So, so far, this is
the problem formulation

00:25:35.770 --> 00:25:38.850
we have been focusing on in the
context of closed loop optimal

00:25:38.850 --> 00:25:45.210
control, discrete dynamics,
and discrete cost.

00:25:45.210 --> 00:25:47.395
Now we go back to the
continuous time formulation.

00:25:47.395 --> 00:25:48.770
It is actually
the one we started

00:25:48.770 --> 00:25:55.210
with when we were talking about
open loop optimal control,

00:25:55.210 --> 00:25:57.450
where now the
dynamics are written

00:25:57.450 --> 00:26:02.490
in terms of a differential
equation x dot equals f of xu,

00:26:02.490 --> 00:26:07.430
and the cost is now
still an additive cost.

00:26:07.430 --> 00:26:11.050
But instead of summation,
we have an integral.

00:26:11.050 --> 00:26:14.370
So this lecture will
be quite interesting.

00:26:14.370 --> 00:26:15.810
It's also quite deep.

00:26:15.810 --> 00:26:18.810
So stay attentive.

00:26:18.810 --> 00:26:23.660
And also there will
be some changes

00:26:23.660 --> 00:26:26.540
of notation across the lecture
for very specific reasons

00:26:26.540 --> 00:26:27.780
that I will discuss.

00:26:27.780 --> 00:26:30.220
So be focused.

00:26:30.220 --> 00:26:32.038
The plus, though, is
that actually this

00:26:32.038 --> 00:26:33.080
is a pretty interesting--

00:26:33.080 --> 00:26:35.372
I mean, all lectures, of
course, are super interesting.

00:26:35.372 --> 00:26:37.420
But this one, I mean,
personally, I find

00:26:37.420 --> 00:26:40.460
it particularly interesting.

00:26:40.460 --> 00:26:45.060
In particular, we're going to
look at a more general setting

00:26:45.060 --> 00:26:47.840
that will become clear
later why we do so.

00:26:50.420 --> 00:26:52.040
That is interesting
in its own right

00:26:52.040 --> 00:26:53.580
and also quite kind
of complementary

00:26:53.580 --> 00:26:55.420
to what we've done so far.

00:26:55.420 --> 00:26:59.540
Specifically, we now assume
that, in addition to everything

00:26:59.540 --> 00:27:05.700
I said so far, our dynamics are
also influenced by a disturbance

00:27:05.700 --> 00:27:08.580
term dt.

00:27:08.580 --> 00:27:11.260
So when we were talking about
discrete time stochastic

00:27:11.260 --> 00:27:15.980
formulation, d was
just a random variable

00:27:15.980 --> 00:27:18.780
generated according to some
probability distribution.

00:27:18.780 --> 00:27:22.240
Here we're are in a
deterministic setting,

00:27:22.240 --> 00:27:27.680
but d encodes the notion
of another player.

00:27:27.680 --> 00:27:32.080
Think of it as nature that
is trying to interfere

00:27:32.080 --> 00:27:35.080
with our objectives.

00:27:35.080 --> 00:27:38.580
These are the so-called
adversarial formulation

00:27:38.580 --> 00:27:41.080
for optimal control
that are very

00:27:41.080 --> 00:27:45.760
useful to come up with control
policies that are really robust.

00:27:45.760 --> 00:27:49.600
No matter what nature throws
at you, within some limit,

00:27:49.600 --> 00:27:51.720
you're still guaranteed
if you use techniques

00:27:51.720 --> 00:27:55.680
that we're going to explain,
to achieve your objectives.

00:27:55.680 --> 00:27:58.937
Of course, the price for it is
that sometimes these policies

00:27:58.937 --> 00:28:00.520
can be quite
conservative because they

00:28:00.520 --> 00:28:03.200
had to protect you
from the worst case

00:28:03.200 --> 00:28:06.800
that nature can throw at you.

00:28:06.800 --> 00:28:09.120
But you see that all these
things are complementary.

00:28:09.120 --> 00:28:14.480
So in many settings, you prefer
a more stochastic formulation.

00:28:14.480 --> 00:28:19.890
Average realization of what
happens is fine enough with you.

00:28:19.890 --> 00:28:23.290
But for example, when
your life is at stake,

00:28:23.290 --> 00:28:25.930
probably you take a
more pessimistic view

00:28:25.930 --> 00:28:27.270
of what could happen.

00:28:27.270 --> 00:28:30.892
So you're not happy anymore with
just reasoning about averages.

00:28:30.892 --> 00:28:33.350
Actually, you want to protect
yourself from the worst case.

00:28:33.350 --> 00:28:35.610
So that's what
formulation is doing.

00:28:35.610 --> 00:28:40.690
Player 1, we control
u tau with u.

00:28:40.690 --> 00:28:44.470
So you're controlling
u to maximize j.

00:28:44.470 --> 00:28:48.390
So here is a little bit of
a difference in objective.

00:28:48.390 --> 00:28:51.290
So now j, even though
it's written as j,

00:28:51.290 --> 00:28:53.490
plays the role of a reward.

00:28:53.490 --> 00:28:54.910
So you want to maximize j.

00:28:54.910 --> 00:28:57.330
You want to make j
as large as possible.

00:28:57.330 --> 00:29:00.730
Think a bit about your worth.

00:29:00.730 --> 00:29:06.130
Player 2, which
use control dt will

00:29:06.130 --> 00:29:10.290
aim to minimize j subject to
the joint system dynamics.

00:29:10.290 --> 00:29:15.350
So u and d are coupled through
the joint system dynamics.

00:29:15.350 --> 00:29:21.660
So the evolution of the
state depends on both u and d

00:29:21.660 --> 00:29:26.780
through the function f, and the
cost g also depends on u and d.

00:29:26.780 --> 00:29:29.140
We'll see some examples later.

00:29:29.140 --> 00:29:31.110
But does this make sense?

00:29:31.110 --> 00:29:32.860
We have generalized a
setting where now we

00:29:32.860 --> 00:29:39.120
have a disturbance, which
is not just any disturbance,

00:29:39.120 --> 00:29:44.500
is actually an active player
that is trying to damage you.

00:29:44.500 --> 00:29:48.220
One example that we will revisit
later, and I also mentioned it

00:29:48.220 --> 00:29:55.160
before is that of the
homicidal chauffeur.

00:30:05.580 --> 00:30:06.840
So this is a person.

00:30:06.840 --> 00:30:10.600
So that's you, player 1.

00:30:14.380 --> 00:30:15.770
And here there is a car.

00:30:20.390 --> 00:30:22.490
Well, I mean, my drawing
skills are quite limited.

00:30:22.490 --> 00:30:23.610
Anyway, this is a car.

00:30:27.190 --> 00:30:33.210
And so the pedestrian can
move in any direction.

00:30:36.470 --> 00:30:38.810
But the speed is
in general lower,

00:30:38.810 --> 00:30:40.910
so its top speed
is in general lower

00:30:40.910 --> 00:30:43.950
than the top speed of the car.

00:30:43.950 --> 00:30:48.150
The car is faster
than a pedestrian,

00:30:48.150 --> 00:30:51.790
but it has constraints
on its motion.

00:30:51.790 --> 00:30:54.670
Particularly, a car
cannot move laterally.

00:30:54.670 --> 00:30:56.118
That's why parking is annoying.

00:30:56.118 --> 00:30:57.243
If you can move laterally--

00:30:57.243 --> 00:30:59.570
I mean, for most cars,
you cannot move laterally.

00:31:02.750 --> 00:31:03.930
So that's player 2.

00:31:06.870 --> 00:31:10.150
So ut is essentially
the sequence

00:31:10.150 --> 00:31:12.870
of controls for
your legs in order

00:31:12.870 --> 00:31:15.920
to get out of the
way for the car.

00:31:15.920 --> 00:31:20.520
Dt is the sequence of controls
for the car in terms of throttle

00:31:20.520 --> 00:31:24.040
and steering to try to hit you.

00:31:24.040 --> 00:31:30.080
Now the question is, what is
the set of the joint states?

00:31:30.080 --> 00:31:33.440
So initial conditions for the
pedestrian and initial condition

00:31:33.440 --> 00:31:40.280
for the car, so that you
can survive no matter

00:31:40.280 --> 00:31:43.180
how hard the car is
trying to hit you.

00:31:48.440 --> 00:31:50.160
Just to have a little bit of--

00:31:50.160 --> 00:31:53.040
distance is not in
matters, but it's not

00:31:53.040 --> 00:31:54.760
the only thing that matters.

00:31:54.760 --> 00:31:58.320
Geometry and curvature
also matter quite a bit.

00:31:58.320 --> 00:32:01.800
So, if you want
to save yourself,

00:32:01.800 --> 00:32:06.760
intuitively, what is the best
way to initialize your position?

00:32:06.760 --> 00:32:10.520
So the car is here and
it's a top-down view.

00:32:10.520 --> 00:32:11.580
So this is the car.

00:32:16.810 --> 00:32:21.130
OK, so here are the wheels.

00:32:21.130 --> 00:32:22.950
And can maneuver in this plane.

00:32:22.950 --> 00:32:25.650
And can go backward.

00:32:25.650 --> 00:32:27.370
And you want to survive.

00:32:27.370 --> 00:32:30.650
Where should I drop you?

00:32:30.650 --> 00:32:34.690
Here or here?

00:32:34.690 --> 00:32:35.930
Here.

00:32:35.930 --> 00:32:37.170
Exactly.

00:32:37.170 --> 00:32:38.530
Exactly.

00:32:38.530 --> 00:32:41.650
So now you understand
differential games.

00:32:41.650 --> 00:32:47.230
Because, even though you
are slower since the car,

00:32:47.230 --> 00:32:51.610
it's faster, but it's
also curvature constraint.

00:32:51.610 --> 00:32:54.370
What we'll end up doing, we
start to constantly trying

00:32:54.370 --> 00:32:55.470
to circle around you.

00:32:55.470 --> 00:32:59.530
But you're smart enough to
keep the car going around you.

00:32:59.530 --> 00:33:03.650
Of course, if you are
unfortunately dropped here,

00:33:03.650 --> 00:33:05.710
most likely, there is
not much you can do.

00:33:05.710 --> 00:33:10.170
And no matter how you
maneuver u, at some point,

00:33:10.170 --> 00:33:11.480
the car is going to hit you.

00:33:11.480 --> 00:33:13.147
Now the question is,
how do we formulate

00:33:13.147 --> 00:33:14.980
all of that in a
mathematical form

00:33:14.980 --> 00:33:18.600
so that we can actually
find the controls used?

00:33:18.600 --> 00:33:21.940
And then the subset of the
state space with a guarantee

00:33:21.940 --> 00:33:25.140
that if you start
there, then nothing

00:33:25.140 --> 00:33:26.293
is going to happen to you?

00:33:26.293 --> 00:33:27.960
Why are this problem
formulation useful?

00:33:27.960 --> 00:33:29.500
And then I'll get to you.

00:33:29.500 --> 00:33:32.440
Is for example, for collision
avoidance scenarios,

00:33:32.440 --> 00:33:35.260
say that you want to develop
a collision avoidance

00:33:35.260 --> 00:33:37.540
policy for an
aircraft, and you want

00:33:37.540 --> 00:33:41.300
to be absolutely sure that
other aircrafts are not going to

00:33:41.300 --> 00:33:42.337
collide with you?

00:33:42.337 --> 00:33:44.420
I mean, in reality, the
other aircraft most likely

00:33:44.420 --> 00:33:47.260
are not trying to kill
you intentionally,

00:33:47.260 --> 00:33:49.840
but the other pilots
maybe are distracted.

00:33:49.840 --> 00:33:52.938
So, if you lack a
better model for them,

00:33:52.938 --> 00:33:54.313
you just model
them as adversary,

00:33:54.313 --> 00:33:57.740
you know that if you
really have a policy that

00:33:57.740 --> 00:34:01.260
allows you to save yourself
under the worst possible action,

00:34:01.260 --> 00:34:04.900
then if the other pilot is
distracted, is drunk, or maybe

00:34:04.900 --> 00:34:07.720
is really trying to hit you,
then you're going to survive.

00:34:07.720 --> 00:34:08.560
Yeah.

00:34:08.560 --> 00:34:11.630
[INAUDIBLE] the
rule from t0 to 0.

00:34:11.630 --> 00:34:13.790
Oh, yes.

00:34:13.790 --> 00:34:15.590
So it turns out,
and this will become

00:34:15.590 --> 00:34:23.469
clear later that when we reason
about closed loop optimal

00:34:23.469 --> 00:34:26.210
control and continuous time,
particularly with disturbances,

00:34:26.210 --> 00:34:28.790
these are problems referred
to as differential games.

00:34:28.790 --> 00:34:30.989
They're games because
there are two players.

00:34:30.989 --> 00:34:34.909
Differential because the
actions between the two players

00:34:34.909 --> 00:34:38.310
are constrained by the
differential dynamics.

00:34:38.310 --> 00:34:43.270
In those settings, we like to
think about the final time as 0

00:34:43.270 --> 00:34:47.449
and initial time t0 as some
negative time in the past.

00:34:47.449 --> 00:34:50.469
So V of t0 as minus 5, minus 6.

00:34:50.469 --> 00:34:51.770
This is really notation.

00:34:51.770 --> 00:34:54.949
And this is a typical notation
used in the differential game

00:34:54.949 --> 00:34:56.110
literature.

00:34:56.110 --> 00:34:57.490
So it's really notation.

00:34:57.490 --> 00:34:58.910
There is nothing deeper here.

00:34:58.910 --> 00:35:00.850
But it's good that
you pointed out.

00:35:00.850 --> 00:35:06.430
So for this lecture, the final
time will be denoted by 0.

00:35:06.430 --> 00:35:08.730
So the terminal
condition will be at 0

00:35:08.730 --> 00:35:10.990
and we start in the past.

00:35:10.990 --> 00:35:11.490
Yeah.

00:35:11.490 --> 00:35:14.270
If we use this formulation to
model stochastic environments,

00:35:14.270 --> 00:35:18.090
why do we assume that
a stochastic element

00:35:18.090 --> 00:35:18.950
is an adversary.

00:35:18.950 --> 00:35:27.330
[INAUDIBLE] in a way
that goes further

00:35:27.330 --> 00:35:30.370
away from us, and therefore
these are [INAUDIBLE].

00:35:30.370 --> 00:35:33.470
Yeah, I mean, this is just
an adversarial formulation.

00:35:33.470 --> 00:35:35.230
And the problem
remains deterministic.

00:35:35.230 --> 00:35:37.010
So there is no
stochasticity here.

00:35:37.010 --> 00:35:39.730
But you could also consider
stochastic formulation.

00:35:39.730 --> 00:35:42.530
Actually, [INAUDIBLE], which is
the next generation collision

00:35:42.530 --> 00:35:48.370
avoidance system, is built
upon stochastic formulation.

00:35:48.370 --> 00:35:50.850
And then, of course, you
can capture some notion

00:35:50.850 --> 00:35:53.930
of adversarial behavior through
the probability distribution

00:35:53.930 --> 00:35:56.330
that defines the realization
of the random variable.

00:35:56.330 --> 00:35:59.650
These are different
modeling choices.

00:35:59.650 --> 00:36:01.790
What I'm telling you is
I'm giving you a menu,

00:36:01.790 --> 00:36:03.650
I mean, that's the
point of this class,

00:36:03.650 --> 00:36:05.530
of different control
methodologies

00:36:05.530 --> 00:36:07.940
and how to solve for them.

00:36:07.940 --> 00:36:11.460
But then, I mean, you're
still the agentic agent

00:36:11.460 --> 00:36:14.620
that has to decide
what to do, at least

00:36:14.620 --> 00:36:19.100
for the foreseeable future.

00:36:19.100 --> 00:36:20.940
Now what interesting
part, and here

00:36:20.940 --> 00:36:25.060
is when you have to apply
your brain is the problem

00:36:25.060 --> 00:36:28.540
as described here is
not fully specified,

00:36:28.540 --> 00:36:33.580
meaning what type of information
does player 2 have access to

00:36:33.580 --> 00:36:37.820
in order to respond
to what you do?

00:36:37.820 --> 00:36:40.020
And there are a
few formulations.

00:36:40.020 --> 00:36:43.740
The most intuitive one,
but also a very bad one,

00:36:43.740 --> 00:36:49.260
is that I as player 1
declare my full control

00:36:49.260 --> 00:36:53.660
sequence from time t0 all
the way to the final time 0.

00:36:53.660 --> 00:36:56.160
Player 2 gets to see that.

00:36:56.160 --> 00:37:02.100
And then it optimizes its own
optimal control sequence dt.

00:37:02.100 --> 00:37:05.390
Of course, this gives an
enormous advantage to player

00:37:05.390 --> 00:37:07.150
2 because he knows
exactly what you're

00:37:07.150 --> 00:37:10.790
going to do between
now and the final time.

00:37:10.790 --> 00:37:17.110
So this formulation typically
makes the problem very simple,

00:37:17.110 --> 00:37:22.110
because essentially, the nested
optimization between what

00:37:22.110 --> 00:37:26.330
the player does and the player
1 gets very easily decoupled.

00:37:26.330 --> 00:37:29.390
So you just look at all
possible control actions

00:37:29.390 --> 00:37:31.770
that you could entail.

00:37:31.770 --> 00:37:33.903
And then I look how to
optimally respond to them.

00:37:33.903 --> 00:37:36.070
And then I solve that
optimization problem and done.

00:37:36.070 --> 00:37:39.530
Computationally cheap, but very
unrealistic, very conservative

00:37:39.530 --> 00:37:44.190
because you give an enormous
advantage to the other player.

00:37:44.190 --> 00:37:48.630
The one we look into is
a bit more sophisticated.

00:37:48.630 --> 00:37:54.310
So here the player
2 gets to see what

00:37:54.310 --> 00:37:57.390
player 1 has done up until now.

00:37:57.390 --> 00:38:01.030
So it does not have
an anticipatory skill

00:38:01.030 --> 00:38:04.120
of also seeing what you
will be doing in the future.

00:38:04.120 --> 00:38:07.520
So it's a constant game
between player 1 and player 2.

00:38:07.520 --> 00:38:09.700
The player makes a
choice, player 2 see that.

00:38:09.700 --> 00:38:11.700
And then they keep
iterating in closed loop.

00:38:11.700 --> 00:38:12.680
Does that make sense?

00:38:12.680 --> 00:38:14.097
It's a little bit
of a difference,

00:38:14.097 --> 00:38:16.240
but it's very profound.

00:38:16.240 --> 00:38:18.800
So the other agent,
meaning player 2,

00:38:18.800 --> 00:38:23.840
acts based on state and control
trajectory up to current time.

00:38:23.840 --> 00:38:28.320
Note, and the notation is that
the set of strategies we look at

00:38:28.320 --> 00:38:32.700
are those strategies whereby
we get to see u from time t0

00:38:32.700 --> 00:38:34.280
up to the current time.

00:38:34.280 --> 00:38:37.720
Note the player 2 still has
a little bit of advantage

00:38:37.720 --> 00:38:40.840
because it gets to see
first, what you do now

00:38:40.840 --> 00:38:42.720
and then a reply.

00:38:42.720 --> 00:38:43.500
Think about chess.

00:38:43.500 --> 00:38:45.240
I mean, usually white
has an advantage

00:38:45.240 --> 00:38:46.640
because it moves first.

00:38:46.640 --> 00:38:49.320
So here player 2
still has an advantage

00:38:49.320 --> 00:38:51.920
because the current time t.

00:38:51.920 --> 00:38:55.200
You player 1 make a move
and then I make my move.

00:38:55.200 --> 00:38:57.960
So I get to react.

00:38:57.960 --> 00:39:00.760
So actually, an inverse
of chess, actually.

00:39:00.760 --> 00:39:03.970
So, does it make sense?

00:39:03.970 --> 00:39:05.810
So this is the
class of strategies

00:39:05.810 --> 00:39:07.810
we're going to focus on.

00:39:07.810 --> 00:39:10.690
Those that do not
anticipate, which

00:39:10.690 --> 00:39:14.570
of course will be infeasible in
practice, what the player 1 will

00:39:14.570 --> 00:39:18.810
do, so is much closer to
the type of operations

00:39:18.810 --> 00:39:20.550
you will see in practice.

00:39:25.770 --> 00:39:28.410
Any questions here?

00:39:28.410 --> 00:39:29.190
This is important.

00:39:29.190 --> 00:39:31.590
So if you have any
questions on this part.

00:39:35.890 --> 00:39:39.450
So if you have a
static game theory,

00:39:39.450 --> 00:39:41.190
this is game theory on steroids.

00:39:41.190 --> 00:39:43.690
Because now there
is a game aspect.

00:39:43.690 --> 00:39:47.010
But now you also have to think
about the differential evolution

00:39:47.010 --> 00:39:50.010
of the dynamics, which makes the
problem even more interesting

00:39:50.010 --> 00:39:52.093
because now there are
geometric considerations you

00:39:52.093 --> 00:39:54.090
have to consider and so on.

00:39:54.090 --> 00:39:59.010
So the problem we want to
solve then is as follows.

00:39:59.010 --> 00:40:04.460
We want to solve a
minimax problem, whereby

00:40:04.460 --> 00:40:08.740
the player 1 will obtain
to maximize its outcome.

00:40:08.740 --> 00:40:11.360
Again, here we are in a setting
where we view j as a reward.

00:40:11.360 --> 00:40:15.380
So you're player 1 and you
try to optimize your outcome.

00:40:15.380 --> 00:40:18.020
And player 2 will
aim to minimize

00:40:18.020 --> 00:40:23.340
it subject to the
system dynamics f.

00:40:23.340 --> 00:40:25.940
So we want to find
a u that makes

00:40:25.940 --> 00:40:30.140
the cost as high as
possible under the most

00:40:30.140 --> 00:40:32.800
damaging disturbance d.

00:40:41.180 --> 00:40:42.220
Make sense?

00:40:42.220 --> 00:40:43.540
Yeah.

00:40:43.540 --> 00:40:45.480
[INAUDIBLE] into the
semantics a little bit.

00:40:45.480 --> 00:40:47.960
But I'm curious,
on the prior slide,

00:40:47.960 --> 00:40:50.820
you said that player
2 has the advantage.

00:40:50.820 --> 00:40:54.300
I'm starting to see how player
2 would have the advantage when

00:40:54.300 --> 00:40:56.220
player 1 would be
[INAUDIBLE] first

00:40:56.220 --> 00:40:59.070
in this structure [INAUDIBLE].

00:40:59.070 --> 00:41:04.110
Because, essentially, I
get to see what you do.

00:41:04.110 --> 00:41:10.990
And so, say that, for
example, there are two cars.

00:41:10.990 --> 00:41:16.750
So this player 1
and this player 2.

00:41:16.750 --> 00:41:21.710
In a dynamic situation, say
the player 2 wants to hit you.

00:41:25.832 --> 00:41:28.430
So you're player 2.

00:41:28.430 --> 00:41:32.310
So say that you know at
time t, player 1 is going

00:41:32.310 --> 00:41:34.043
to move in this direction.

00:41:34.043 --> 00:41:35.710
This is a very useful
information to you

00:41:35.710 --> 00:41:38.550
because you know that these
directions are not useful to you

00:41:38.550 --> 00:41:39.950
to damage player 1.

00:41:39.950 --> 00:41:44.670
Actually, going this
direction is advantageous.

00:41:44.670 --> 00:41:49.350
So you see how having access to
what the other person is going

00:41:49.350 --> 00:41:52.910
to do, basically,
allows you to rule out

00:41:52.910 --> 00:41:55.810
actions that are not going
to be maximally damaging,

00:41:55.810 --> 00:41:59.720
and instead focus on the ones
that are maximally damaging.

00:41:59.720 --> 00:42:02.200
Does that make sense?

00:42:02.200 --> 00:42:04.240
So in this problem,
are we solving

00:42:04.240 --> 00:42:07.880
for the worst disturbances
or the optimal control?

00:42:07.880 --> 00:42:08.920
Both.

00:42:08.920 --> 00:42:09.420
Yeah.

00:42:09.420 --> 00:42:10.880
That's why it's minimax term.

00:42:10.880 --> 00:42:11.940
We want to--

00:42:16.120 --> 00:42:18.220
I mean, at the end of the
day, you care about you.

00:42:18.220 --> 00:42:22.400
But in doing so, you solve
simultaneously with respect

00:42:22.400 --> 00:42:25.800
to both the optimal disturbance
and the optimal control.

00:42:25.800 --> 00:42:30.640
So you try to find the u that
makes the cost, as I said,

00:42:30.640 --> 00:42:35.640
as high as possible under the
worst possible disturbance t.

00:42:35.640 --> 00:42:38.840
And you compute both of
them, of course, in practice,

00:42:38.840 --> 00:42:42.280
unless you take the
role of player 2,

00:42:42.280 --> 00:42:44.520
you're going to
use the control u.

00:42:44.520 --> 00:42:47.640
What's the notation [INAUDIBLE]?

00:42:47.640 --> 00:42:48.140
Yeah.

00:42:48.140 --> 00:42:52.640
So this is a abstract notation
that says that we pick,

00:42:52.640 --> 00:42:57.180
that is only those disturbance
d that are not anticipated.

00:42:57.180 --> 00:43:04.580
So they can only react to what
player 1 has done up until now.

00:43:04.580 --> 00:43:06.880
So it doesn't have-- is
what I was saying before,

00:43:06.880 --> 00:43:12.980
doesn't have access to what
player 1 will do in the future.

00:43:12.980 --> 00:43:16.380
Player 1 looks at the time
history of player 2, which

00:43:16.380 --> 00:43:17.860
is not [INAUDIBLE].

00:43:17.860 --> 00:43:18.520
That's right.

00:43:18.520 --> 00:43:23.020
So it's only
instantaneous advantage.

00:43:23.020 --> 00:43:23.660
Great.

00:43:23.660 --> 00:43:25.468
So that's it.

00:43:25.468 --> 00:43:27.760
So the problem formulation
here is quite sophisticated.

00:43:27.760 --> 00:43:28.260
Yeah.

00:43:28.260 --> 00:43:29.860
This might have
been said already.

00:43:29.860 --> 00:43:35.180
But why is the player
1 with the control

00:43:35.180 --> 00:43:39.120
you're trying to maximize
cost instead minimize it?

00:43:39.120 --> 00:43:39.620
No.

00:43:39.620 --> 00:43:42.620
So what I said is that
in this specific setting,

00:43:42.620 --> 00:43:45.380
don't view g as a cost.

00:43:45.380 --> 00:43:48.460
View g as a reward.

00:43:48.460 --> 00:43:48.960
Oh.

00:43:48.960 --> 00:43:49.877
It's just terminology.

00:43:49.877 --> 00:43:52.220
So here, there is
nothing deep here.

00:43:52.220 --> 00:43:55.150
So we are in a reward setting
but using the g notation

00:43:55.150 --> 00:43:58.176
because unfortunately, that's
what the community uses.

00:43:58.176 --> 00:44:00.690
[INAUDIBLE] around
the min and max,

00:44:00.690 --> 00:44:03.310
like, find the minimum
possible disturbance,

00:44:03.310 --> 00:44:05.430
and then find a control
that would, like,

00:44:05.430 --> 00:44:07.870
given that disturbance,
be most optimal?

00:44:07.870 --> 00:44:11.390
No, actually, to give the
