00:45:00.410 --> 00:45:07.400
Well, I think This business
is best interpreted when

00:45:07.400 --> 00:45:09.160
you are at a general time t.

00:45:09.160 --> 00:45:18.520
And so you have some history
of xp2 tau and x1, 1 tau.

00:45:18.520 --> 00:45:23.560
So in this [INAUDIBLE]
just a general time t,

00:45:23.560 --> 00:45:27.240
having access to the
information, including time t--

00:45:27.240 --> 00:45:30.818
basically, strictly speaking,
you have more information

00:45:30.818 --> 00:45:33.360
because you have the additional
bit of information at time t.

00:45:33.360 --> 00:45:36.560
And having more information
is always more useful.

00:45:36.560 --> 00:45:37.518
Does that make sense?

00:45:37.518 --> 00:45:40.060
In general, more information is
better than less information.

00:45:40.060 --> 00:45:42.340
So we can also justify
it in that way.

00:45:44.780 --> 00:45:45.280
Great.

00:45:45.280 --> 00:45:45.820
All right.

00:45:45.820 --> 00:45:48.240
So how do we solve this problem?

00:45:48.240 --> 00:45:54.130
As usual, dynamic programming
and principle of optimality.

00:45:54.130 --> 00:45:56.310
So the solution is as follows.

00:45:59.330 --> 00:46:12.410
We decompose the objective
into two pieces--

00:46:12.410 --> 00:46:17.970
immediate reward
plus reward to go.

00:46:17.970 --> 00:46:21.370
And remember, from when we were
talking about the principle

00:46:21.370 --> 00:46:25.250
optimality, that there
is no loss in optimality

00:46:25.250 --> 00:46:28.290
if we restrict the search
of the optimal control

00:46:28.290 --> 00:46:32.130
signals to only those
control signals that results

00:46:32.130 --> 00:46:40.810
from the concatenation
of optimal tails and then

00:46:40.810 --> 00:46:44.610
immediate rewards.

00:46:44.610 --> 00:46:48.570
So this is represented
notionally by the drawing

00:46:48.570 --> 00:46:52.140
here where I'm showing
ensuring that if I give you

00:46:52.140 --> 00:46:55.820
the optimal solution
for the tail problem

00:46:55.820 --> 00:47:02.540
starting at time t plus t
at state xt plus delta t,

00:47:02.540 --> 00:47:05.380
you don't need to reason
about control sequences

00:47:05.380 --> 00:47:09.620
to do something else starting
from point B3 or point B2

00:47:09.620 --> 00:47:10.800
or point B1.

00:47:10.800 --> 00:47:14.760
You just concatenate the initial
segment with the optimal tails,

00:47:14.760 --> 00:47:17.620
and you just compare the
three control sequences that

00:47:17.620 --> 00:47:19.360
arise from this concatenations.

00:47:22.900 --> 00:47:26.260
So here, I'm enforcing the
principle of optimality

00:47:26.260 --> 00:47:30.800
in this decomposition
of the cost.

00:47:30.800 --> 00:47:32.360
So here, I'm not
doing anything new.

00:47:32.360 --> 00:47:33.735
I'm really applying
the principle

00:47:33.735 --> 00:47:36.060
of optimality in the
same shape and form

00:47:36.060 --> 00:47:41.260
that we discussed
a few lectures ago.

00:47:41.260 --> 00:47:45.420
So the point is that
the problem here, which

00:47:45.420 --> 00:47:48.520
is the original formulation,
h of x here, as usual,

00:47:48.520 --> 00:47:54.710
is the terminal cost,
can be rewritten

00:47:54.710 --> 00:47:58.390
as the cost whereby
[INAUDIBLE] concatenate

00:47:58.390 --> 00:48:05.330
optimal tales with the, in
a way, one step transition.

00:48:05.330 --> 00:48:08.270
So view basically this integral
between t and plus delta t

00:48:08.270 --> 00:48:13.750
has a very short transition
into the new time.

00:48:13.750 --> 00:48:17.550
So if you have fully grasped
the principle of optimality,

00:48:17.550 --> 00:48:20.010
then this should be quite clear.

00:48:20.010 --> 00:48:21.650
If this doesn't
look quite clear,

00:48:21.650 --> 00:48:23.990
it means that you haven't
analyzed yet the principle

00:48:23.990 --> 00:48:24.688
optimality.

00:48:24.688 --> 00:48:26.730
So try to do a little bit
more thinking about it.

00:48:30.230 --> 00:48:33.030
As I said a couple of lectures
ago, the principle of optimality

00:48:33.030 --> 00:48:36.310
is such that when you
see the first time,

00:48:36.310 --> 00:48:37.910
you think it's obvious.

00:48:37.910 --> 00:48:40.590
After a while, it
becomes very confusing,

00:48:40.590 --> 00:48:43.590
and after a little while
then becomes obvious again.

00:48:43.590 --> 00:48:46.410
So when I see it,
it's obvious to me.

00:48:46.410 --> 00:48:48.780
But there was a time where
it was not so obvious to me.

00:48:48.780 --> 00:48:50.920
So I'm trying to say
it takes a bit of time

00:48:50.920 --> 00:48:54.280
for you to really
internalize it.

00:48:54.280 --> 00:49:00.200
Anyway, so assuming that we
buy this step where we enforce

00:49:00.200 --> 00:49:01.920
the principle of
optimality, so we just

00:49:01.920 --> 00:49:05.600
say that we look at
control and disturbance

00:49:05.600 --> 00:49:08.800
sequences that are the
results of the concatenation

00:49:08.800 --> 00:49:10.380
of optimal tails.

00:49:10.380 --> 00:49:16.200
And first bit of
control between time

00:49:16.200 --> 00:49:21.080
t to t plus delta t, how we
can massage these expressions

00:49:21.080 --> 00:49:24.880
into something that leads me to
something that is computable.

00:49:24.880 --> 00:49:29.740
So view delta t as a
small time increment.

00:49:32.618 --> 00:49:34.160
By the way, this
stuff mathematically

00:49:34.160 --> 00:49:35.060
is very complicated.

00:49:35.060 --> 00:49:37.850
So if you were to do it
super, super cleanly,

00:49:37.850 --> 00:49:39.100
it would be a bit complicated.

00:49:39.100 --> 00:49:42.080
Here, I'm just giving
you the main intuition.

00:49:42.080 --> 00:49:46.010
This is probably one of
the most complex concepts

00:49:46.010 --> 00:49:48.890
in optimal control.

00:49:48.890 --> 00:49:55.010
So the integral can be
approximated as g times delta t.

00:49:55.010 --> 00:49:57.890
So a very, very small
interval delta t,

00:49:57.890 --> 00:50:01.210
you can view the function
g almost a constant value.

00:50:01.210 --> 00:50:06.490
So the integral is simply equal
to the integrand time, the time

00:50:06.490 --> 00:50:07.570
difference delta t.

00:50:10.306 --> 00:50:15.130
xt plus delta t can
be written according

00:50:15.130 --> 00:50:20.850
to one step Euler discretization
as xt plus delta t f of xud.

00:50:20.850 --> 00:50:23.850
I mean, we have done
this many, many times,

00:50:23.850 --> 00:50:29.090
which means that we can
approximate the optimal cost

00:50:29.090 --> 00:50:33.210
to go from at time
t plus delta t--

00:50:33.210 --> 00:50:34.910
I'll rewrite it here--

00:50:37.930 --> 00:50:44.310
with a once first order
Taylor series approximation.

00:50:44.310 --> 00:50:53.190
But this becomes j of xdt plus
gradient of j with respect

00:50:53.190 --> 00:51:02.190
to x times x of t plus
delta t minus x of t.

00:51:02.190 --> 00:51:11.270
But this is equal
to delta tf of xud

00:51:11.270 --> 00:51:15.070
plus plus partial
derivative of j with respect

00:51:15.070 --> 00:51:16.570
to t times delta t.

00:51:19.750 --> 00:51:23.190
So this is a simple step.

00:51:23.190 --> 00:51:28.990
Here, there is the crux of the
problem, the most difficult

00:51:28.990 --> 00:51:33.310
thing to swallow in
this entire lecture.

00:51:33.310 --> 00:51:36.050
So we have jj.

00:51:36.050 --> 00:51:39.310
So, nothing new here.

00:51:39.310 --> 00:51:42.630
We have approximated
the integral

00:51:42.630 --> 00:51:45.400
with just this quantity
that we have here.

00:51:45.400 --> 00:51:49.920
We have substituted the j
of xt plus delta t comma

00:51:49.920 --> 00:51:54.300
t plus delta t with its
Taylor series expansion.

00:51:54.300 --> 00:52:01.600
OK, so we have it here.

00:52:01.600 --> 00:52:08.620
But now the part that is tricky
is this instantaneous scale.

00:52:08.620 --> 00:52:15.800
So now I have to remove u
and d as a function of time

00:52:15.800 --> 00:52:17.580
over a continuous interval.

00:52:20.120 --> 00:52:25.800
Now d and u are evaluated
exactly at the time t.

00:52:25.800 --> 00:52:29.280
So at this instantaneous
scale, the disturbance

00:52:29.280 --> 00:52:33.320
is not choosing a whole
trajectory anymore.

00:52:33.320 --> 00:52:37.180
It is choosing a reaction
to the current control.

00:52:41.120 --> 00:52:46.250
So the next structure changes
from the outer player picking

00:52:46.250 --> 00:52:49.490
a function, anticipating
the optimal response,

00:52:49.490 --> 00:52:53.550
to at each instant, one player
moves and the other reacts.

00:52:53.550 --> 00:52:56.370
So this becomes closer
to chess, actually.

00:52:56.370 --> 00:53:02.650
So that's why we swap the
order between min and max--

00:53:02.650 --> 00:53:06.330
to preserve the advantage with
respect to the disturbance.

00:53:06.330 --> 00:53:08.110
That's the part that
is hard to swallow.

00:53:08.110 --> 00:53:10.370
It would require a
much deeper proof.

00:53:10.370 --> 00:53:12.130
But the idea is that
essentially, we're

00:53:12.130 --> 00:53:14.890
trying to be consistent
with our assumption

00:53:14.890 --> 00:53:22.410
that player two is the one
that has the advantage.

00:53:26.874 --> 00:53:29.930
[INAUDIBLE] give the
control and then [INAUDIBLE]

00:53:29.930 --> 00:53:32.090
reacts optimally.

00:53:32.090 --> 00:53:36.770
And so the problem changes
from min max to max min.

00:53:36.770 --> 00:53:41.820
So this is not
entirely obvious, and I

00:53:41.820 --> 00:53:44.000
don't want to spend
too much time on it

00:53:44.000 --> 00:53:46.460
because otherwise, it will
be an entire lecture on it.

00:53:46.460 --> 00:53:50.320
So I'm trying to give you as
much intuition as possible.

00:53:50.320 --> 00:53:54.300
Here, it might be a little
bit cloudy what is happening.

00:53:54.300 --> 00:53:56.137
But the point being
is that in order

00:53:56.137 --> 00:53:58.220
to be consistent with the
fact that player two has

00:53:58.220 --> 00:54:02.220
an advantage, we have,
when the problem boils down

00:54:02.220 --> 00:54:05.065
to instantaneous
optimization, swap

00:54:05.065 --> 00:54:07.440
the notion of a minimum and
maximum, maximum and minimum.

00:54:13.820 --> 00:54:16.820
This is the hardest
part to swallow.

00:54:16.820 --> 00:54:19.180
So here's a little bit
of a step where you

00:54:19.180 --> 00:54:20.880
have to trust your instructor.

00:54:20.880 --> 00:54:25.780
But at least trust me in the
sense that what we're doing here

00:54:25.780 --> 00:54:27.780
is trying to be consistent
with the assumption

00:54:27.780 --> 00:54:31.660
that the player two
has the advantage.

00:54:31.660 --> 00:54:35.380
So the idea is that
we are player one.

00:54:35.380 --> 00:54:39.830
We fix u, and then
d gets to choose

00:54:39.830 --> 00:54:41.650
the possible best response.

00:54:41.650 --> 00:54:46.070
And then you try to pick
the u such that for that u,

00:54:46.070 --> 00:54:51.362
d has the less
damaging response.

00:54:51.362 --> 00:54:53.070
But player two is an
advantage because he

00:54:53.070 --> 00:54:54.130
gets to see what you do.

00:54:57.510 --> 00:55:01.870
OK, so assuming we agree for
this step-- and from now on,

00:55:01.870 --> 00:55:04.970
actually, the lecture
will be downhill.

00:55:04.970 --> 00:55:07.310
So it's uphill because the
formulation of differential

00:55:07.310 --> 00:55:09.350
games is a little
bit complicated

00:55:09.350 --> 00:55:13.590
because we have this business
of non-anticipatory strategies

00:55:13.590 --> 00:55:18.350
that is relatively natural
but not straightforward.

00:55:18.350 --> 00:55:21.950
It gets to the peak, where these
swapping between min max and max

00:55:21.950 --> 00:55:28.590
Min is a bit deep as a concept.

00:55:28.590 --> 00:55:30.330
But then it gets
downhill from now on.

00:55:30.330 --> 00:55:31.430
Yeah.

00:55:31.430 --> 00:55:31.930
Sorry.

00:55:31.930 --> 00:55:37.480
So why couldn't we write
max min since the beginning?

00:55:37.480 --> 00:55:41.080
Because here, this min
max and this max min

00:55:41.080 --> 00:55:44.760
has a bit of different
interpretation.

00:55:44.760 --> 00:55:47.720
Here, we're doing
nested optimization

00:55:47.720 --> 00:55:50.840
over the space of
control sequences.

00:55:50.840 --> 00:55:53.360
Here, we're doing
nested optimization

00:55:53.360 --> 00:55:55.840
over space of
instantaneous control.

00:55:55.840 --> 00:55:58.080
So within this
terminology, actually, it

00:55:58.080 --> 00:56:00.840
turns out that to give
[INAUDIBLE] an advantage,

00:56:00.840 --> 00:56:03.960
it has to be part of
the outer approximation.

00:56:03.960 --> 00:56:08.120
That's the part where
it can be understood,

00:56:08.120 --> 00:56:10.120
but it will require a bit
of a deeper discussion

00:56:10.120 --> 00:56:11.377
to be super rigorous.

00:56:11.377 --> 00:56:12.460
So that's what I'm saying.

00:56:12.460 --> 00:56:14.293
So it's a little bit
of one of the few parts

00:56:14.293 --> 00:56:18.200
in the class where you have
to trust your instructor.

00:56:18.200 --> 00:56:19.660
The most important
thing, though,

00:56:19.660 --> 00:56:21.618
is that you understand
the problem formulation,

00:56:21.618 --> 00:56:25.920
and then you understand
the final algorithm

00:56:25.920 --> 00:56:27.047
that we're going to derive.

00:56:27.047 --> 00:56:28.380
That's the most important thing.

00:56:28.380 --> 00:56:30.463
Here, I'm just providing
a little bit of intuition

00:56:30.463 --> 00:56:32.380
about what that
algorithm is coming from.

00:56:32.380 --> 00:56:35.440
The specific steps
are not so essential.

00:56:35.440 --> 00:56:41.410
Anyway, assuming we agree on
this, then we arrive here.

00:56:41.410 --> 00:56:43.470
So j does not depend on u.

00:56:43.470 --> 00:56:44.970
So I can bring-- md.

00:56:44.970 --> 00:56:48.370
So I can bring you out
of the optimization.

00:56:48.370 --> 00:56:52.530
So these two terms cancel.

00:56:52.530 --> 00:56:53.950
Great.

00:56:53.950 --> 00:56:57.330
Then I can also cancel
these terms delta t.

00:57:01.058 --> 00:57:03.970
OK, and then I get
to the celebrated

00:57:03.970 --> 00:57:06.330
Hamilton-Jacobi-Isaacs equation.

00:57:06.330 --> 00:57:11.210
So the optimal
value function j is

00:57:11.210 --> 00:57:15.330
the solution to a partial
differential equation.

00:57:15.330 --> 00:57:16.830
That seems like bad news.

00:57:16.830 --> 00:57:20.130
Partial differential equations
typically are hard to solve.

00:57:20.130 --> 00:57:23.210
And so basically, if you want to
find the optimal value function

00:57:23.210 --> 00:57:25.688
j, you have to solve the
partial differential equation

00:57:25.688 --> 00:57:27.730
because you're taking the
derivative with respect

00:57:27.730 --> 00:57:30.290
to both time and state.

00:57:30.290 --> 00:57:32.530
And the solution, this
partial differential equation

00:57:32.530 --> 00:57:36.980
on top of it, has a max
min problem embedded in it.

00:57:36.980 --> 00:57:38.548
So, looks ugly.

00:57:38.548 --> 00:57:40.340
And actually, it's not
particularly pretty.

00:57:40.340 --> 00:57:41.715
But there are
solution algorithms

00:57:41.715 --> 00:57:43.640
to solve this partial
differential equation.

00:57:43.640 --> 00:57:45.420
We'll get to those later.

00:57:45.420 --> 00:57:47.740
But at least now, you
have a mathematical tool

00:57:47.740 --> 00:57:52.860
that allows to solve
for the value function.

00:57:52.860 --> 00:57:55.380
And once you have
the value function j,

00:57:55.380 --> 00:57:58.340
then you get your
optimal control u

00:57:58.340 --> 00:58:02.150
by just doing an argmax
of all this quantity here.

00:58:02.150 --> 00:58:03.900
Of course, a partial
differential equation

00:58:03.900 --> 00:58:05.358
[INAUDIBLE] a
differential equation

00:58:05.358 --> 00:58:07.780
needs to have a boundary
condition, which is represented

00:58:07.780 --> 00:58:12.180
by your terminal cost.

00:58:12.180 --> 00:58:16.100
So I'll come back to
this later, but first, I

00:58:16.100 --> 00:58:21.660
want to go back to the setting
of standard optimal control,

00:58:21.660 --> 00:58:23.878
when we do not have
the disturbance.

00:58:23.878 --> 00:58:25.420
So we could have
done the same thing,

00:58:25.420 --> 00:58:27.460
and it would have
been even simpler.

00:58:27.460 --> 00:58:33.120
In that case, you get to the
continuous time formulation

00:58:33.120 --> 00:58:37.080
of the Bellman equation,
which is still--

00:58:37.080 --> 00:58:41.463
we don't have d anymore because
we had removed the disturbance.

00:58:41.463 --> 00:58:43.880
So we are again in a setting
where the dynamics are simply

00:58:43.880 --> 00:58:46.800
x dot equals f of xu.

00:58:46.800 --> 00:58:48.980
We are-- remember, now
we're in continuous time.

00:58:48.980 --> 00:58:52.640
So this is the
continuous time extension

00:58:52.640 --> 00:58:54.080
to the Bellman equation.

00:58:54.080 --> 00:58:56.020
It is still a partial
differential equation.

00:58:56.020 --> 00:58:59.543
You take the derivative
with respect to t and x.

00:58:59.543 --> 00:59:01.960
Again, you don't have the ds
because here, we are assuming

00:59:01.960 --> 00:59:05.200
that there is no disturbance.

00:59:05.200 --> 00:59:08.960
But here, at least I can
give you an intuition

00:59:08.960 --> 00:59:11.535
about this equation represents.

00:59:11.535 --> 00:59:13.160
And then when I give
you the intuition,

00:59:13.160 --> 00:59:15.720
you say, oh, but this
actually is quite obvious.

00:59:15.720 --> 00:59:21.320
Specifically, let's rewrite
the Hamilton-Jacobi-Bellman.

00:59:21.320 --> 00:59:24.720
Hamilton-Jacobi-Bellman is when
we don't have a player two.

00:59:24.720 --> 00:59:30.240
Hamilton-Jacobi-Isaacs is
when we have a player two.

00:59:30.240 --> 00:59:33.410
So let me rewrite this
equation as minus partial

00:59:33.410 --> 00:59:43.450
of j with respect to t equal
minimum over U of g of xut

00:59:43.450 --> 00:59:48.010
plus partial derivative of J
with respect to x of f of xut.

00:59:53.970 --> 00:59:55.610
So if you look at
this, doesn't it

00:59:55.610 --> 00:59:59.580
look like the Bellman
equation discrete time?

00:59:59.580 --> 01:00:01.050
Sort of.

01:00:01.050 --> 01:00:03.490
On the right hand side,
you have the minimization

01:00:03.490 --> 01:00:07.370
over u like the Bellman
equation in discrete time.

01:00:07.370 --> 01:00:12.770
And then inside, you
have the immediate reward

01:00:12.770 --> 01:00:13.610
or immediate cost.

01:00:13.610 --> 01:00:15.470
Depends on how you
interpret this stuff.

01:00:18.130 --> 01:00:19.930
So let's interpret
this as now a cost.

01:00:19.930 --> 01:00:21.930
I told you that there's
going to be a little bit

01:00:21.930 --> 01:00:23.810
of a shift in terminology.

01:00:23.810 --> 01:00:25.530
So that's now why
we're minimizing

01:00:25.530 --> 01:00:28.190
over u-- because we want to make
the cost as small as possible.

01:00:31.060 --> 01:00:35.340
And this is the cost to go.

01:00:35.340 --> 01:00:36.900
And essentially,
it's telling you

01:00:36.900 --> 01:00:40.380
how the cost will
go changes as you

01:00:40.380 --> 01:00:44.100
are moving your state one
step ahead in the direction

01:00:44.100 --> 01:00:46.820
of your dynamics.

01:00:46.820 --> 01:00:49.860
And this is the partial
[INAUDIBLE] with respect to t

01:00:49.860 --> 01:00:54.340
is the time rate of change
of your optimal cost to go.

01:00:54.340 --> 01:00:58.620
So what this equation is telling
you is that minus the time

01:00:58.620 --> 01:01:01.340
rate of change of the
optimal cost to go

01:01:01.340 --> 01:01:09.060
is equal to the minimum over u
of the [INAUDIBLE] cost plus how

01:01:09.060 --> 01:01:13.580
your cost will be changing
when choosing the control u,

01:01:13.580 --> 01:01:15.720
the state will move
into a new state.

01:01:15.720 --> 01:01:18.780
Of course, you're not moving
into a new state arbitrarily.

01:01:18.780 --> 01:01:21.480
You're moving into a new
state following the dynamics.

01:01:21.480 --> 01:01:25.460
That's why the derivative is
taking along your dynamics.

01:01:25.460 --> 01:01:28.710
So in continuous time,
the Bellman equation

01:01:28.710 --> 01:01:32.630
takes the notion of a time rate
of change of your cost function.

01:01:32.630 --> 01:01:34.790
Well, in discrete time,
it was a discrete change

01:01:34.790 --> 01:01:36.830
across iterations.

01:01:36.830 --> 01:01:40.630
Does it shed a bit of light
about what it is coming from?

01:01:40.630 --> 01:01:42.430
So where there is a minus?

01:01:42.430 --> 01:01:45.030
Well, we are now in
a situation whereby

01:01:45.030 --> 01:01:46.530
j is interpreted as a cost.

01:01:46.530 --> 01:01:48.670
We try to minimize the cost.

01:01:48.670 --> 01:01:53.950
So as the horizon shrinks,
there is less cost

01:01:53.950 --> 01:01:56.990
that you're going to accumulate
until the end of time.

01:01:56.990 --> 01:01:58.550
And so your cost is
going to decrease

01:01:58.550 --> 01:02:00.230
as you move forward in time.

01:02:00.230 --> 01:02:01.850
So it makes sense
there is a minus.

01:02:04.430 --> 01:02:06.390
Just to give you guys
as much intuition

01:02:06.390 --> 01:02:09.530
that I didn't get when these
results were presented to me,

01:02:09.530 --> 01:02:12.070
and I was utterly confused
about where all of this

01:02:12.070 --> 01:02:13.830
was coming from.

01:02:13.830 --> 01:02:18.250
So all I'm saying here is that
you shouldn't be too scared,

01:02:18.250 --> 01:02:23.182
at least from the
Hamilton-Jacobi-Bellman equation

01:02:23.182 --> 01:02:27.240
is really very similar to the
discrete time Bellman equation.

01:02:27.240 --> 01:02:29.380
But now, since we are
in continuous time,

01:02:29.380 --> 01:02:34.440
we reason in terms
of time change of--

01:02:34.440 --> 01:02:38.760
rate of change of quantities
in time and with respect

01:02:38.760 --> 01:02:42.000
to how we're moving
our dynamics, our state

01:02:42.000 --> 01:02:45.240
around according
to our dynamics.

01:02:45.240 --> 01:02:47.920
In the adversarial
setting, the intuition

01:02:47.920 --> 01:02:50.220
is a bit more complicated.

01:02:50.220 --> 01:02:50.720
Yeah.

01:02:50.720 --> 01:02:56.820
[INAUDIBLE] u to ensure there's
no change in the cost function.

01:02:56.820 --> 01:02:57.560
Like you're--

01:02:57.560 --> 01:02:58.960
No.

01:02:58.960 --> 01:03:00.633
Why would you change any cost?

01:03:00.633 --> 01:03:02.800
It seems like you're trying
to [INAUDIBLE] u so that

01:03:02.800 --> 01:03:03.580
you're like--

01:03:03.580 --> 01:03:07.640
No, you want to make the change
the rate in terms of time

01:03:07.640 --> 01:03:10.960
and change as large as
possible so that you decrease

01:03:10.960 --> 01:03:14.480
the cost as much as possible.

01:03:14.480 --> 01:03:16.100
That's why you're
minimizing over u.

01:03:19.546 --> 01:03:21.920
OK, so any questions here?

01:03:21.920 --> 01:03:24.800
So main point is that we
set up the optimal control

01:03:24.800 --> 01:03:27.410
problem in continuous time.

01:03:27.410 --> 01:03:29.947
Actually, in a more
general version,

01:03:29.947 --> 01:03:31.530
where we also consider
the possibility

01:03:31.530 --> 01:03:33.130
of a second player
that is trying

01:03:33.130 --> 01:03:36.010
to tamper with our objectives.

01:03:36.010 --> 01:03:39.090
We discussed how we had to
play some assumptions in terms

01:03:39.090 --> 01:03:43.610
of how the player two
can select its decisions.

01:03:43.610 --> 01:03:50.050
We argue how a good model is to
assume that the player two is

01:03:50.050 --> 01:03:53.550
restricted to take actions
that are not anticipatory.

01:03:53.550 --> 01:03:57.050
So it can only get to see what
you have done so far, including

01:03:57.050 --> 01:03:58.450
the current time.

01:03:58.450 --> 01:04:01.170
That gives a slight
advantage to player two,

01:04:01.170 --> 01:04:05.010
which is captured by a specific
formulation of the problem

01:04:05.010 --> 01:04:10.290
where we do a min max.

01:04:10.290 --> 01:04:14.570
Then, given that formulation,
we invoked arguments

01:04:14.570 --> 01:04:18.130
from the principle of optimality
to break down that problem

01:04:18.130 --> 01:04:19.770
into the solution
of a problem whereby

01:04:19.770 --> 01:04:25.500
we concatenate current decisions
with optimal decisions.

01:04:25.500 --> 01:04:30.660
Using that insight, then using
first order approximations,

01:04:30.660 --> 01:04:33.820
with a little bit
of magic and proof

01:04:33.820 --> 01:04:35.780
by authority where
I tell you it's true

01:04:35.780 --> 01:04:41.180
and you are not allowed
to counter-argue

01:04:41.180 --> 01:04:45.200
we derive an equation that is
eventually what we care about,

01:04:45.200 --> 01:04:47.300
which is the
Hamilton-Jacobi-Isaacs

01:04:47.300 --> 01:04:50.820
equation for the differential
gain formulation of the problem

01:04:50.820 --> 01:04:53.838
and the Hamilton-Jacobi-Bellman
equation for the more

01:04:53.838 --> 01:04:56.380
standard version of the problem
where there is no player two.

01:04:56.380 --> 01:04:57.838
And for the
Hamilton-Jacobi-Bellman

01:04:57.838 --> 01:05:01.460
equation, I gave you a little
bit of intuition showcasing that

01:05:01.460 --> 01:05:05.660
even though the equation
looks ugly-- and it is ugly.

01:05:05.660 --> 01:05:08.300
But it does have some
intuition, particularly

01:05:08.300 --> 01:05:12.340
in terms of embodying the notion
of the discrete time Bellman

01:05:12.340 --> 01:05:17.820
equation but in terms
of rate of change.

01:05:17.820 --> 01:05:21.860
That's basically what you
should internalize from here.

01:05:21.860 --> 01:05:24.970
If you want to get a bit deeper
about this topic, as I said,

01:05:24.970 --> 01:05:27.590
it gets mathematically
quite involved.

01:05:27.590 --> 01:05:34.950
Then I provided some
references in the earlier here.

01:05:34.950 --> 01:05:37.750
Ah, these couple of papers
go in much greater detail

01:05:37.750 --> 01:05:39.050
about these derivations.

01:05:41.610 --> 01:05:42.110
Great.

01:05:42.110 --> 01:05:44.010
So now we have all
of these tools.

01:05:44.010 --> 01:05:48.310
Let's start applying these
tools, first to LQR and later,

01:05:48.310 --> 01:05:52.770
meaning Monday, to a class
of problem that is very cool,

01:05:52.770 --> 01:05:56.590
referred to as
reachability problems.

01:05:56.590 --> 01:06:00.910
So next time, we'll wrap up
this lecture with an application

01:06:00.910 --> 01:06:03.590
to reachability problems,
and then we'll move to MPC.

01:06:03.590 --> 01:06:04.490
Yeah.

01:06:04.490 --> 01:06:08.430
In this model, [INAUDIBLE]
you can see the [INAUDIBLE],

01:06:08.430 --> 01:06:09.030
but can you--

01:06:09.030 --> 01:06:09.730
Can you see it?

01:06:09.730 --> 01:06:10.230
Cannot--

01:06:10.230 --> 01:06:14.910
[INAUDIBLE] what,
like-- can you see

01:06:14.910 --> 01:06:17.710
based on what makes more sense?

01:06:17.710 --> 01:06:18.210
No.

01:06:18.210 --> 01:06:19.960
Meaningful deterministic
setting, so there

01:06:19.960 --> 01:06:21.550
is no notion of
stochastic prediction.

01:06:21.550 --> 01:06:22.470
[INAUDIBLE]

01:06:22.470 --> 01:06:22.970
Yeah.

01:06:22.970 --> 01:06:24.970
There are other formulations,
but basically, you

01:06:24.970 --> 01:06:29.050
can have a probabilistic
prediction of what player

01:06:29.050 --> 01:06:29.683
one could do.

01:06:29.683 --> 01:06:31.350
But that's not part
of this formulation.

01:06:31.350 --> 01:06:34.010
OK, so it's only
[INAUDIBLE] player 1.

01:06:34.010 --> 01:06:37.330
[INAUDIBLE] yeah.

01:06:37.330 --> 01:06:42.030
All right, so let's apply
now this stuff to LQR.

01:06:42.030 --> 01:06:45.130
So turns out that the 15th
version of LQR we're going

01:06:45.130 --> 01:06:48.570
to see in this class is
the one in continuous time.

01:06:48.570 --> 01:06:52.090
So now the dynamics are
still linear, time-varying.

01:06:52.090 --> 01:06:55.570
But they are given by
differential equations

01:06:55.570 --> 01:06:57.810
as opposed to discrete
time equation.

01:06:57.810 --> 01:06:59.210
The cost is quadratic.

01:06:59.210 --> 01:07:02.890
By standard having a summation,
now you have an integral.

01:07:02.890 --> 01:07:06.410
Same intuition about the
parameters for LQR problem.

01:07:06.410 --> 01:07:09.930
q penalizes deviation with
respect to the state. r

01:07:09.930 --> 01:07:13.170
penalizes control effort
assumed to be positive definite.

01:07:13.170 --> 01:07:18.090
These units here are specified,
and there are no constraints

01:07:18.090 --> 01:07:21.260
on the state and the control.

01:07:21.260 --> 01:07:25.500
So can you solve LQR by using
the Hamilton-Jacobi-Bellman

01:07:25.500 --> 01:07:26.160
equation?

01:07:26.160 --> 01:07:27.000
Yes, you can.

01:07:27.000 --> 01:07:29.400
I'm not going to do
the full derivation.

01:07:29.400 --> 01:07:32.525
It's not particularly
insightful.

01:07:32.525 --> 01:07:34.400
For those of you that
are interested, though,

01:07:34.400 --> 01:07:38.220
the way that it would
work is as follows.

01:07:38.220 --> 01:07:44.140
So we know from the discrete
time formulation of LQR

01:07:44.140 --> 01:07:48.180
that the optimal cost to go
is a quadratic in the state.

01:07:48.180 --> 01:07:50.320
So you start with an ansatz.

01:07:50.320 --> 01:07:53.220
That's how oftentimes, you solve
partial differential equation.

01:07:53.220 --> 01:07:55.620
You start with an ansatz,
which is basically

01:07:55.620 --> 01:07:59.540
the cool mathematical
term for a guess, right?

01:07:59.540 --> 01:08:03.740
So you start with a guess
that your optimal cost to go

01:08:03.740 --> 01:08:10.060
is a quadratic for some
matrix v that you do not know.

01:08:10.060 --> 01:08:11.380
That's a guess.

01:08:11.380 --> 01:08:14.800
You plug it into the partial
differential equation,

01:08:14.800 --> 01:08:17.060
and then you see if
there are some conditions

01:08:17.060 --> 01:08:21.790
on b that makes the partial
differential equation satisfied.

01:08:21.790 --> 01:08:26.050
So you do a bunch of boring
algebraic manipulations,

01:08:26.050 --> 01:08:30.069
and you'll see
that if v is chosen

01:08:30.069 --> 01:08:33.390
as the solution of this
differential equation

01:08:33.390 --> 01:08:35.410
where everything is
known-- so we know q.

01:08:35.410 --> 01:08:36.470
We know b.

01:08:36.470 --> 01:08:40.510
We know a, and then we
have the boundary condition

01:08:40.510 --> 01:08:42.470
that b at the final
time is equal to h.

01:08:42.470 --> 01:08:46.189
So if v is solved according
to this differential

01:08:46.189 --> 01:08:48.750
equation referred to as
the continuous time Riccati

01:08:48.750 --> 01:08:50.630
equation, then the
partial differential

01:08:50.630 --> 01:08:53.670
equation the
Hamilton-Jacobi-Bellman partial

01:08:53.670 --> 01:08:55.649
differential
equation, is solved,

01:08:55.649 --> 01:08:59.670
which means that your guess was
correct, taking into account

01:08:59.670 --> 01:09:02.710
that v is chosen as follows.

01:09:02.710 --> 01:09:04.790
So, I mean, quite quick,
but the main point

01:09:04.790 --> 01:09:08.750
is that the way that the
solution for LQR will be derived

01:09:08.750 --> 01:09:12.470
is by guessing what the
optimal cost is parameterized

01:09:12.470 --> 01:09:14.910
with respect to a matrix v.

01:09:14.910 --> 01:09:17.180
Plug it into the
Hamilton-Bellman equation.

01:09:17.180 --> 01:09:19.960
See if there is an
appropriate choice of v such

01:09:19.960 --> 01:09:22.380
that the partial differential
equation is satisfied

01:09:22.380 --> 01:09:24.859
because if you know that
it is satisfied, indeed,

01:09:24.859 --> 01:09:27.700
the j is the optimal cost to go.

01:09:27.700 --> 01:09:29.520
It turns out that
if you choose v

01:09:29.520 --> 01:09:32.160
as the solution of this
ordinary differential equation,

01:09:32.160 --> 01:09:34.080
that's indeed the case.

01:09:34.080 --> 01:09:37.560
And then the control
is given by--

01:09:37.560 --> 01:09:41.040
surprise, surprise-- linear
feedback on the state

01:09:41.040 --> 01:09:47.600
now with a slightly different
set of multiplications.

01:09:47.600 --> 01:09:50.040
So that's all I wanted
to say about LQR.

01:09:50.040 --> 01:09:53.640
Main point is that you
would solve it still

01:09:53.640 --> 01:09:56.050
through the lens of
dynamic programming.

01:09:56.050 --> 01:09:57.800
Now it's a bit more
complicated because we

01:09:57.800 --> 01:10:00.040
have to solve the partial
differential equation.

01:10:00.040 --> 01:10:02.400
But once you make
this guess, it's

01:10:02.400 --> 01:10:04.280
not actually too complicated.

01:10:04.280 --> 01:10:07.680
And the main takeaways are that
the optimal cost is quadratic

01:10:07.680 --> 01:10:14.000
and the optimal control is still
linear feedback on the state.

01:10:14.000 --> 01:10:18.370
In the notes you
find all the details.

01:10:18.370 --> 01:10:21.850
Instead, what I want to do is
to focus more on applications

01:10:21.850 --> 01:10:23.770
of differential games.

01:10:23.770 --> 01:10:25.930
So the differential
game formulation,

01:10:25.930 --> 01:10:28.050
the formulation where you
also have a player two

01:10:28.050 --> 01:10:30.650
is actually quite useful
in a number of settings.

01:10:30.650 --> 01:10:33.830
There are a number of so-called
pursuit evasion games,

01:10:33.830 --> 01:10:37.050
so games similar to the
homicidal chauffeur problem

01:10:37.050 --> 01:10:38.890
that I mentioned before.

01:10:38.890 --> 01:10:45.170
And our broad set of
problems that actually

01:10:45.170 --> 01:10:46.970
are the ones we're
going to focus on

01:10:46.970 --> 01:10:49.138
are referred to as
reachability problems.

01:10:49.138 --> 01:10:50.930
And then there are also
many other problems

01:10:50.930 --> 01:10:53.370
in economics and such
that lead themselves

01:10:53.370 --> 01:10:56.292
quite well to differential
game formulation.

01:10:56.292 --> 01:10:58.250
But in this class, I want
to focus a little bit

01:10:58.250 --> 01:11:03.850
on reachability analysis that
you might have heard maybe.

01:11:03.850 --> 01:11:07.170
So in theory, we're
interested in computing

01:11:07.170 --> 01:11:12.650
the so-called backward reachable
set of a dynamical system.

01:11:12.650 --> 01:11:16.700
So this is the set of states
such that the trajectories

01:11:16.700 --> 01:11:22.180
that start from this set are
guaranteed to reach a given

01:11:22.180 --> 01:11:26.480
set at a final time tf.

01:11:26.480 --> 01:11:30.780
So say that you
specify a given set.

01:11:30.780 --> 01:11:37.780
The question is, if you start
one minute in the past, what

01:11:37.780 --> 01:11:40.460
is the set of
initial states such

01:11:40.460 --> 01:11:44.140
that you are guaranteed to
land into the target set

01:11:44.140 --> 01:11:45.240
at the present time.

01:11:49.060 --> 01:11:52.820
So again, the reachable
set, backward reachable set,

01:11:52.820 --> 01:11:55.700
is the set of states such that
trajectories start from this set

01:11:55.700 --> 01:11:57.920
can reach some given target set.

01:12:01.460 --> 01:12:06.100
Now, depending on how you
interpret the target set

01:12:06.100 --> 01:12:10.780
that you want to reach, then
you have different flavors

01:12:10.780 --> 01:12:12.590
of backward reachability.

01:12:12.590 --> 01:12:14.950
In particular, the target
set could be something

01:12:14.950 --> 01:12:16.990
that you want to avoid.

01:12:16.990 --> 01:12:20.830
So basically, you want
to find the set of states

01:12:20.830 --> 01:12:30.110
that because of an adversarial
choice or controls by player two

01:12:30.110 --> 01:12:34.070
will lead to that avoidance set.

01:12:34.070 --> 01:12:36.750
You want to then
start outside of that

01:12:36.750 --> 01:12:41.150
set so that you're
not guaranteed to hit

01:12:41.150 --> 01:12:42.450
an obstacle, for example.

01:12:45.510 --> 01:12:48.990
So this will be the case
of an avoidance set.

01:12:48.990 --> 01:12:53.910
So the target set is
basically when you hit a tree,

01:12:53.910 --> 01:12:58.510
and the blue shape would
encapsulate all the states such

01:12:58.510 --> 01:13:02.870
that if you start here,
no matter what you do,

01:13:02.870 --> 01:13:06.390
there is going to be a
strategy for the player 2--

01:13:06.390 --> 01:13:11.480
for example, a fairly
unfortunate sequence of wind

01:13:11.480 --> 01:13:16.000
gusts that, boom, will bring
you into the avoidance set.

01:13:16.000 --> 01:13:17.460
Why is that useful for control?

01:13:17.460 --> 01:13:19.360
For example, for
planning algorithms?

01:13:19.360 --> 01:13:22.200
Because then, you formulate
your planning algorithm such

01:13:22.200 --> 01:13:28.840
that you never start from
states that are in within here.

01:13:28.840 --> 01:13:31.000
But sometimes, the
target set actually

01:13:31.000 --> 01:13:32.460
has a more benign notion.

01:13:32.460 --> 01:13:37.000
It could be the set that
you want to get into.

01:13:37.000 --> 01:13:41.240
In the case, we talk about
the goal-reaching problems.

01:13:41.240 --> 01:13:43.320
The backward reachable
set now has the notion

01:13:43.320 --> 01:13:46.960
of the set of states
such that no matter how

01:13:46.960 --> 01:13:49.480
player two is trying
to screw this up,

01:13:49.480 --> 01:13:53.160
we can still reach that set.

01:13:53.160 --> 01:13:58.720
And so for planning purposes,
you want to start inside here.

01:13:58.720 --> 01:14:02.920
So depending on how you
interpret the target set,

01:14:02.920 --> 01:14:07.400
you may want to start as player
one either inside or outside

01:14:07.400 --> 01:14:09.637
of it.

01:14:09.637 --> 01:14:11.220
Now, then, the
question is, how do you

01:14:11.220 --> 01:14:15.960
compute these sets
here depicted in blue?

01:14:15.960 --> 01:14:17.760
That's the problem with
attribute analysis.

01:14:17.760 --> 01:14:19.218
That's the problem
to which we will

01:14:19.218 --> 01:14:24.460
apply Hamilton-Jacobi-Isaacs
argument.

01:14:24.460 --> 01:14:27.160
So let me just state the
problem more formally here.

01:14:27.160 --> 01:14:31.000
We'll apply the machinery from
this lecture to these problems

01:14:31.000 --> 01:14:31.760
next time.

01:14:31.760 --> 01:14:33.540
I will also have an
interactive script

01:14:33.540 --> 01:14:36.940
that will allow us to play a
little bit with these problems.

01:14:36.940 --> 01:14:45.820
So to recap, the backward
reachable set in the case that

01:14:45.820 --> 01:14:49.980
the target set corresponds to
danger-- so we don't get there--

01:14:49.980 --> 01:14:55.420
is the set of states
x bar such that there

01:14:55.420 --> 01:14:59.860
exists a player two
strategy so that no matter

01:14:59.860 --> 01:15:04.220
what we do as player one,
under the joint dynamics,

01:15:04.220 --> 01:15:08.470
we are going to hit
the undesired target

01:15:08.470 --> 01:15:11.750
at the final time,
which is time zero.

01:15:11.750 --> 01:15:20.710
Conversely, the goal-reaching
formulation is--

01:15:20.710 --> 01:15:23.070
the goal-reachable
set rt is the set

01:15:23.070 --> 01:15:28.430
of states such that no matter
what player two is trying to do,

01:15:28.430 --> 01:15:32.270
there exists a control
action as player 1

01:15:32.270 --> 01:15:35.690
that I can enact such that
under the joint dynamics,

01:15:35.690 --> 01:15:37.590
I'm going to end
up in that state.

01:15:37.590 --> 01:15:39.190
So I'm happy.

01:15:39.190 --> 01:15:40.790
So these are the two
class of problems

01:15:40.790 --> 01:15:43.890
that we're going to look at.

01:15:43.890 --> 01:15:47.070
So we'll see on Monday how
to apply the techniques we

01:15:47.070 --> 01:15:48.050
discussed today.

01:15:48.050 --> 01:15:50.750
It will take about
20, 25 minutes.

01:15:50.750 --> 01:15:52.650
But you see this is
actually quite useful.

01:15:52.650 --> 01:15:55.990
You can use it quite
a bit in a variety

01:15:55.990 --> 01:15:58.830
of robotics applications,
applications

01:15:58.830 --> 01:16:01.670
